  "@graph": [
    {
      "@id": "urn:robos:elearning:course:getting-started-with-robos",
      "@type": [
        "oslc_am:Resource",
        "robos:ELearningCourse",
        "robos:ELearning",
        "schema:Course"
      ],
      "dcterms:description": "Your first hour or so with RobOS, written for complete beginners. Install one app, run a pretend coffee shop, find the file your work is saved in, back it up and restore it on a fresh machine. Then learn how optional AI works and what changing an app really takes today. Real screen recordings, hands-on steps and quizzes that explain every answer.",
      "dcterms:title": "Getting Started with RobOS",
      "robos:difficulty": "Beginner",
      "robos:domainStandard": "https://schema.org/Course",
      "robos:estimatedDuration": "80 minutes",
      "robos:featured": true,
      "robos:gitopsFile": ".robos/elearning.yaml",
      "robos:modules": [
        {
          "id": "gs-01-what-robos-is",
          "title": "Lesson 1: What RobOS is, in plain English",
          "durationMinutes": 6,
          "overview": "RobOS is a free collection of apps that run on your own computer. This lesson explains what that means, the few words you need, and the small example you will follow through the whole course.",
          "body": "## The short version\n\nRobOS is a set of more than 100 free apps for your computer: a cash register for a shop, a car maintenance log, a music player, a password vault, games, and a large toolbox for software developers. You install only the ones you want.\n\nThree things make it different from the apps you may pay for today:\n\n- **It costs nothing.** There is no subscription. The code is public under the Apache-2.0 open-source license.\n- **Your work stays on your computer.** Apps save to files on your own disk. There is no RobOS account and no RobOS server holding your data.\n- **AI is optional.** Every app in this course works with no AI at all. Lesson 6 covers the features that can use it.\n\n> **An honest note.** RobOS is young: the current version is 0.0.10. Some apps are far along and some are early. RobOS Household, for example, is a practice sandbox that uses invented family data and cannot hold your real finances. Try an app with sample data before you rely on it.\n\n## The words you need\n\n| Word | What it means |\n|---|---|\n| App Manager | The one program you download first. You use it to install and remove every other app. |\n| Open source | Anyone can read and change the code. |\n| Local | Saved on your own computer, not on a company's server. |\n| Backup vault | A copy of your RobOS files, kept in a folder you choose. You will make one in lesson 5. |\n| AI agent | A separate AI program, such as Claude Code or Codex, that can carry out tasks. You bring your own if you want one. |\n| Terminal | A window where you type commands. You only need one for the optional parts of lesson 7. |\n\n## The example we will follow\n\nReading about software is hard to remember, so you will do one small thing from start to finish. You will pretend to run **Bean There Coffee**, an invented coffee shop that comes with the RobOS Point of Sale app. You will never need a real shop, and no real money moves.\n\n| Lesson | What you will have done |\n|---|---|\n| 2 | Installed App Manager and the Point of Sale app |\n| 3 | Sold a latte and given correct change |\n| 4 | Added a new drink, then found the file your shop is saved in |\n| 5 | Backed the shop up, and seen how to bring it back on a new computer |\n| 6 | Learned how optional AI works and how RobOS screens what you send |\n| 7 | Learned how the app's own code can change today, and who that suits |\n| 8 | Chosen what to try next |\n\n## What you need\n\n- A computer running Windows, macOS or Linux. Ubuntu with the GNOME desktop is the best-tested Linux.\n- An internet connection for the downloads. The first app took about 1.2 MB when we tested it.\n- No account, no payment card, no AI and no coding.\n",
          "keyPoints": [
            "RobOS is free apps that run on your computer and save to files on your disk.",
            "There is no RobOS account or server, and AI is optional.",
            "You will follow one invented coffee shop, Bean There Coffee, through the course."
          ],
          "labSteps": [
            "Pick the one app you most want to try.",
            "Check that your computer is supported."
          ],
          "labDetails": [
            {
              "description": "Look through the [App Directory](https://rowbose.com/apps/). You will still use Point of Sale for this course, because it needs nothing but itself. Note the other app for lesson 8.",
              "expected": "You have one app in mind, for example Auto Center or Audio Player."
            },
            {
              "description": "Open the [download page](https://rowbose.com/download/) and find the file for your computer. Do not install it yet. If you do not know which Mac you have, open the Apple menu and choose About This Mac: an M-series chip means Apple Silicon.",
              "expected": "You can name your file: the Windows installer, the Ubuntu .deb, the Linux AppImage, or the Apple Silicon or Intel DMG."
            }
          ],
          "quiz": [
            {
              "question": "A friend asks where RobOS Point of Sale keeps their sales. What do you tell them?",
              "options": [
                "In a file on the computer running the app",
                "In a RobOS cloud account they sign in to",
                "On a server that RobOS rents for each shop",
                "Only in memory until they buy a storage plan"
              ],
              "answer": "In a file on the computer running the app",
              "explanation": "RobOS has no server or account. Apps save to files on your own disk, and lesson 4 shows you the exact file."
            },
            {
              "question": "You never set up any AI. What happens to Point of Sale?",
              "options": [
                "It works as normal, because AI is optional",
                "It opens in a limited demo mode",
                "It asks you to subscribe before starting",
                "It keeps working for a trial period only"
              ],
              "answer": "It works as normal, because AI is optional",
              "explanation": "Nothing in this course up to lesson 6 uses AI. RobOS apps do not depend on it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "gs-02-install",
          "title": "Lesson 2: Install App Manager and your first app",
          "durationMinutes": 10,
          "overview": "You download one small program, App Manager, and use it to install only the apps you choose. By the end of this lesson, Point of Sale is on your computer.",
          "body": "## Why there are two steps\n\nMost software arrives as one big download. RobOS has more than 100 apps, and you probably want three of them. So you first install **App Manager**, a small program whose only job is to fetch and remove the others. Think of it as a small app store with no shop attached: nothing costs money and nothing needs an account.\n\n## Step 1: Install App Manager\n\nDownload the file for your computer from [rowbose.com/download](https://rowbose.com/download/).\n\n| Your computer | The file | How to install it |\n|---|---|---|\n| Windows | `Windows-Setup.exe` | Open it and follow the installer. |\n| Ubuntu GNOME | `Linux-amd64.deb` | Double-click it and choose **Install**, then enter your password. |\n| Other Linux | `Linux-x86_64.AppImage` | Make it executable and open it. It needs FUSE 2, which is the `libfuse2t64` package on Ubuntu. |\n| Mac with Apple Silicon | `macOS-arm64.dmg` | Open it and drag RobOS to Applications. |\n| Mac with an Intel chip | `macOS-x64.dmg` | Open it and drag RobOS to Applications. |\n\nPrefer the terminal on Ubuntu? From the folder holding the download, and using the file's real version number, this does the same as double-clicking:\n\n```bash\nsudo apt install ./RobOS-v0.0.10-Linux-amd64.deb\n```\n\n## Step 2: Choose an app\n\nOpen **RobOS App Manager**. The list is split into groups: Everyday & business, Developer tools, AI & agents, Games & game tools, and System & security. Tick the box beside **Point of Sale**. The bottom of the window tells you how much will download before you commit to anything.\n\n![App Manager with Point of Sale ticked, showing 1 app selected, 1.2 MB to download and 5.9 MB on disk](/assets/elearning/getting-started/manager-selected.png)\n\nTicking a group's own box selects every app in it. You do not want that today: it is an easy way to download 62 developer tools by accident.\n\n## Step 3: Install it\n\nPress **Install selected**. Here is the real thing, sped up:\n\n![App Manager downloading Point of Sale until the message Selected apps are ready to open appears](/assets/elearning/getting-started/manager-install.gif)\n\nWhen it finishes you will see **Selected apps are ready to open**, and an **Open** button on the app's row. The app also gets its own launcher, so you never need App Manager to start it:\n\n| Your computer | Where the app's launcher appears |\n|---|---|\n| Ubuntu GNOME | A **RobOS Apps** folder in the app grid |\n| Windows | **RobOS Apps** in the Start menu |\n| macOS | `~/Applications/RobOS Apps` |\n| Other Linux | Your desktop's app menu |\n\n## Removing things later\n\nTick an installed app and choose **Uninstall selected**. **Uninstall RobOS…** at the bottom left removes App Manager and every app it installed.\n\nEither way, your data is kept. That is deliberate: reinstalling picks up where you left off. It also means uninstalling does not erase your files, so do not count on it to clean a computer you are giving away.\n\n## If something goes wrong\n\n| What you see | What to do |\n|---|---|\n| \"App catalog unavailable\" | App Manager could not reach the download list. Check your internet connection and press **Refresh**. |\n| The AppImage will not open | Install FUSE 2. On Ubuntu that is `sudo apt install libfuse2t64`. |\n| You can't see **Install selected** | Tick an app first. If you tick an app that is already installed and has an update, the button says **Apply updates**. |\n| The list looks empty | Press **Refresh**, and clear the **Search apps** box. |\n",
          "keyPoints": [
            "App Manager is the one thing you download. It installs everything else.",
            "Tick only what you want; the window shows the download size before you commit.",
            "Each app gets its own launcher, and uninstalling keeps your data."
          ],
          "labSteps": [
            "Install App Manager.",
            "Install Point of Sale.",
            "Open it from its own launcher."
          ],
          "labDetails": [
            {
              "description": "Download the file you found in lesson 1 from [the download page](https://rowbose.com/download/) and install it using the table above.",
              "expected": "RobOS App Manager opens and shows a list of more than 100 applications."
            },
            {
              "description": "Type **Point of Sale** in the **Search apps** box, tick it, and press **Install selected**.",
              "expected": "The window says **Selected apps are ready to open**, and the **Installed** count on the left shows 1.",
              "image": "/assets/elearning/getting-started/manager-installed.png",
              "imageAlt": "App Manager showing Point of Sale installed, with Open and Uninstall buttons on its row"
            },
            {
              "description": "Close App Manager. Find Point of Sale in your app grid, Start menu or Applications folder and open it from there.",
              "expected": "A window opens with the heading **Welcome to RobOS Point of Sale**. Leave it for the next lesson."
            }
          ],
          "quiz": [
            {
              "question": "You only want the cash register. What do you have to download?",
              "options": [
                "App Manager, then just Point of Sale",
                "Every RobOS app, then hide the others",
                "The Developer tools group, then Point of Sale",
                "A full RobOS operating system image"
              ],
              "answer": "App Manager, then just Point of Sale",
              "explanation": "App Manager fetches only the apps you tick. The operating system image is a separate option for a dedicated computer, and you do not need it here."
            },
            {
              "question": "You uninstall Point of Sale and reinstall it next month. What happens to your shop?",
              "options": [
                "It is still there, because uninstalling keeps your data",
                "It is gone, because the app and its data go together",
                "It is restored from a RobOS server",
                "It moved to the Trash, and you must restore it first"
              ],
              "answer": "It is still there, because uninstalling keeps your data",
              "explanation": "Uninstalling removes the program and its launchers. Your data files stay in place, so reinstalling picks up where you left off."
            },
            {
              "question": "App Manager says \"App catalog unavailable\". What is the most likely fix?",
              "options": [
                "Check your connection, then press Refresh",
                "Buy a RobOS license for this computer",
                "Delete your data folder and start again",
                "Install every app in one go instead"
              ],
              "answer": "Check your connection, then press Refresh",
              "explanation": "The catalog is the list of downloads, fetched over the internet. No license exists, and nothing about this error involves your data."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "gs-03-first-sale",
          "title": "Lesson 3: Sell your first latte",
          "durationMinutes": 12,
          "overview": "You open a pretend coffee shop, sign in as the cashier and sell one latte with oat milk. Each number on the receipt is explained, so you can check the app got it right.",
          "body": "## What is a point of sale?\n\nA **point of sale** is the till: the thing a cashier uses to add up an order, take payment and print a receipt. RobOS Point of Sale does that on an ordinary computer or touchscreen. It comes with sample businesses so you can practice without setting anything up.\n\n> **Nothing here is real money.** Pressing cash buttons only records numbers on your computer. Nothing is charged to anyone.\n\n## Step 1: Open the coffee shop\n\nOn the welcome screen, click **Coffee shop**. That creates Bean There Coffee, with a full menu and a small team.\n\n![The welcome screen of Point of Sale offering a retail store, a coffee shop or a bar](/assets/elearning/getting-started/pos-first-run.png)\n\n## Step 2: Sign in as the cashier\n\nShops give each worker a short code called a **PIN**, so the till knows who did what, and so a cashier can't do the things only an owner may do. The sample team has three:\n\n| Person | PIN | What they can do |\n|---|---|---|\n| Owner (Olivia) | `1111` | Everything, including changing the menu |\n| Manager | `2222` | Refunds, reports, stock |\n| Cashier (Cleo) | `3333` | Sell, take payment, run the drawer |\n\nEnter `3333` on the number pad and press the button below it. You are now Cleo.\n\n## Step 3: Open the cash drawer\n\nA shop starts each day with some cash in the drawer to give change from. The usual word for it is the **float**. The till needs to know how much that is, so that at closing time it can tell you whether the drawer is short.\n\nClick **Cash**, enter `100`, and press **Open drawer**.\n\n![The Cash screen with $100 entered and an Open drawer button](/assets/elearning/getting-started/pos-open-drawer.png)\n\n## Step 4: Ring up the latte\n\nClick **Register**, then **Latte**. A latte needs a size and a milk, so the **Add** button only works once you have picked both. Choose **Medium**, **Oat** and **Extra shot**. The button now reads **Add · $6.35**. Press it.\n\n![The Latte options with Medium, Oat and Extra shot chosen, and a button reading Add $6.35](/assets/elearning/getting-started/pos-latte-options.png)\n\n## Step 5: Add a tip and take the money\n\nPress **Tip** and choose **20%**. The total becomes **$8.16**. Press **Pay $8.16**. Your customer hands you a $20 bill, so press **$20.00**, then scroll down in that window and press **Take cash**.\n\nThe whole sale takes about 30 seconds. Here it is, sped up:\n\n![The complete sale: signing in, opening the drawer, building the latte, adding a tip, paying and seeing the receipt](/assets/elearning/getting-started/pos-sale.gif)\n\n## Step 6: Check the receipt\n\nNever trust a till just because it looks tidy. Check that you can reach the same numbers by hand:\n\n| Line | Working | Amount |\n|---|---|---|\n| Latte | Menu price | $4.50 |\n| Medium | Add-on | $0.50 |\n| Oat | Add-on | $0.60 |\n| Extra shot | Add-on | $0.75 |\n| Subtotal | The four lines above | $6.35 |\n| Prepared food tax | 8.5% of $6.35, rounded | $0.54 |\n| Tip | 20% of $6.35 | $1.27 |\n| Total | $6.35 + $0.54 + $1.27 | $8.16 |\n| Change | $20.00 − $8.16 | $11.84 |\n\n![The receipt from Bean There Coffee showing a total of $8.16 and change of $11.84](/assets/elearning/getting-started/pos-receipt.png)\n\nPress **New order** to clear the screen. The tax rate and everything else on that receipt are sample values, and you will change some of them in the next lesson.\n\n## What just happened behind the scenes\n\nThe order did not stop at the receipt. Click **Prep**, and you will see order #1 waiting for the barista, who moves it along as it is made and collected. The same sale also feeds the **Orders**, **Inventory** and **Reports** screens. You will read the report in the next lesson.\n",
          "keyPoints": [
            "A sample business lets you practice with no setup and no real money.",
            "PINs decide who you are and what you are allowed to do.",
            "You should be able to work out every number on a receipt by hand."
          ],
          "labSteps": [
            "Open Bean There Coffee and sign in as Cleo.",
            "Sell the latte, with a 20% tip, for $20 cash.",
            "See the order waiting at the Prep screen."
          ],
          "labDetails": [
            {
              "description": "Choose **Coffee shop**, then enter PIN `3333`. Open the drawer from the **Cash** screen with `100`.",
              "expected": "The top right of the window says **Cleo (Cashier)**, and the shop name beside the logo says **Bean There Coffee**."
            },
            {
              "description": "Follow steps 4 and 5 above: Latte, Medium, Oat, Extra shot, a 20% tip, then $20.00 in cash.",
              "expected": "The window says **Paid**, with **Change due $11.84**, and the receipt total is **$8.16**. If your numbers differ, check the size and milk you chose: Small adds nothing, Large adds $1.00."
            },
            {
              "description": "Press **New order**, then click **Prep**.",
              "expected": "You see a ticket for order #1 with the latte on it."
            }
          ],
          "quiz": [
            {
              "question": "The Add button on the latte will not work. What is the most likely reason?",
              "options": [
                "You have not chosen both a size and a milk",
                "The cash drawer has not been counted yet",
                "A cashier is not allowed to sell lattes",
                "The order already has too many add-ons"
              ],
              "answer": "You have not chosen both a size and a milk",
              "explanation": "Size and milk are marked as required on that window. Extras are optional."
            },
            {
              "question": "A latte is $6.35 before tax, and tax is 8.5%. Why does the receipt say $0.54 in tax?",
              "options": [
                "8.5% of $6.35 is about 0.54 once rounded to cents",
                "Tax is a flat 54 cents on every drink",
                "Tax is worked out on the $20 you were handed",
                "Tax is worked out after the tip is added"
              ],
              "answer": "8.5% of $6.35 is about 0.54 once rounded to cents",
              "explanation": "6.35 times 0.085 is 0.53975, which rounds to 54 cents. The tip is not taxed here, and the cash handed over plays no part."
            },
            {
              "question": "Why does the till ask how much cash is in the drawer when you open it?",
              "options": [
                "So it can later tell you if the drawer is over or short",
                "So it can send the amount to your bank",
                "So it can stop you selling more than that",
                "So it can work out the tax on each sale"
              ],
              "answer": "So it can later tell you if the drawer is over or short",
              "explanation": "The float is the starting point. The till adds cash sales to it and compares the result with your count at closing time."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "gs-04-your-data",
          "title": "Lesson 4: Make it yours, and find where it is saved",
          "durationMinutes": 10,
          "overview": "You add a new drink without any code and read the sales report. Then you find the one file on your computer where the whole shop lives, which sets you up for backing it up.",
          "body": "## Add a drink, as the owner\n\nChanging the menu is an owner's job, so Cleo can't do it. Press **Lock** at the top right and enter `1111`. You are now Olivia, and more tabs have appeared. On a smaller window, the row of tabs may scroll sideways.\n\n1. Click **Menu & Catalog**, then **+ Add item**.\n2. Set the **Name** to `Maple Cold Brew`, the **Category** to **Brewed & Cold**, the **Price** to `5.25` and the **Prep station** to `barista`.\n3. Scroll down and press **Save**.\n\n![The Add item window filled in for Maple Cold Brew at 5.25](/assets/elearning/getting-started/pos-add-item.png)\n\nNow click **Register**, then the **Brewed & Cold** filter. Your drink is already on sale. You did not restart anything or write any code.\n\n![The register showing Maple Cold Brew at $5.25 beside the other cold drinks](/assets/elearning/getting-started/pos-new-item.png)\n\nThe same goes for much of the shop: **Settings** holds the business name, address and **Receipt footer**, and **Menu & Catalog** holds taxes, promo codes and happy hours. Most things a shop wants to change are here, not in the code.\n\n## Read the report\n\nClick **Reports**. This is your one latte, from a shop owner's side of the counter:\n\n![The sales report showing 1 order, gross sales $6.35, tax $0.54, tips $1.27 and $8.16 taken in cash](/assets/elearning/getting-started/pos-report.png)\n\nIt's worth a look at why the numbers differ. **Gross sales** is $6.35, because that's what the coffee earned the shop. The $0.54 of tax belongs to the government and the $1.27 of tips usually belongs to the staff. Together they make up the **$8.16** shown under **By tender**, which is the word tills use for how someone paid.\n\n## Where is all this saved?\n\nEverything you have done is in one file on your computer:\n\n| Your computer | The file |\n|---|---|\n| Linux or macOS | `~/.config/robos/point-of-sale/store.json` |\n| Windows | `%USERPROFILE%\\.config\\robos\\point-of-sale\\store.json` |\n\nTwo pieces of shorthand need explaining. `~` means your home folder, so on a Mac it is really `/Users/yourname/.config/...`. And a folder whose name starts with a dot, like `.config`, is **hidden**, which is why you have never noticed it.\n\nHere's how to get there:\n\n- **Ubuntu:** in Files, press `Ctrl+L`, type `~/.config/robos/point-of-sale` and press Enter.\n- **macOS:** in Finder, press `Cmd+Shift+G`, type the same path and press Return.\n- **Windows:** click the address bar in File Explorer, paste `%USERPROFILE%\\.config\\robos\\point-of-sale` and press Enter.\n\nThe file is plain text, in a common format called JSON. It holds the menu, staff, orders and settings. You can look, but do not edit it by hand, least of all while the app is open. PINs are not stored readably: they are scrambled in a way that can check a PIN but not reveal it.\n\n## The rest of your RobOS files\n\n| What | Where | Back it up? |\n|---|---|---|\n| App data and settings | `~/.config/robos/` | Yes |\n| More RobOS data, such as project records | `~/.robos/` | Yes |\n| The apps themselves (on Linux) | `~/.local/share/robos/app-manager/` | No, you can download them again |\n| Passwords in Pass Manager | `~/.config/robos/vault/robos-passwords.kdbx`, a KeePassXC file encrypted with your master password | Yes. It goes into a vault as it is, still encrypted |\n| API keys for AI tools and VPN secrets | `~/.password-store/`, encrypted by a tool called `pass` | Handle separately; Config Sync does not upload these |\n\nYou only need the first row today. The point is that your data is ordinary files you can see, copy and take with you. That is the advantage of an app that keeps everything local, and also the catch: **if this computer dies, so does the only copy.** The next lesson fixes that.\n",
          "keyPoints": [
            "Menus, prices, taxes and receipts are settings you can change yourself, with no code.",
            "Your whole shop is one file: ~/.config/robos/point-of-sale/store.json.",
            "Local files mean you control your data, and that you are responsible for backing it up."
          ],
          "labSteps": [
            "Add Maple Cold Brew as the owner.",
            "Read your sales report.",
            "Find your shop on disk."
          ],
          "labDetails": [
            {
              "description": "Press **Lock**, enter `1111`, and add the item as shown above. If you try the same thing as Cleo, you will find there is no **Menu & Catalog** tab at all.",
              "expected": "**Maple Cold Brew** at **$5.25** appears on the register under **Brewed & Cold**."
            },
            {
              "description": "Click **Reports**. Make sure you can explain why gross sales and the cash taken are different numbers.",
              "expected": "One order, **$6.35** gross sales, **$0.54** tax, **$1.27** tips, and **$8.16** cash under **By tender**."
            },
            {
              "description": "Use the instructions above for your computer to open the folder. Look at the size of `store.json`. Do not edit it.",
              "expected": "You find `store.json`, around 20 KB. Add another sale and its modified time changes."
            }
          ],
          "quiz": [
            {
              "question": "The report says gross sales are $6.35, but the drawer took $8.16. Is something wrong?",
              "options": [
                "No, the extra is $0.54 of tax and $1.27 of tips",
                "Yes, the till has lost track of $1.81 somewhere",
                "No, the extra is the float from the cash drawer",
                "Yes, the tax was charged twice on this order"
              ],
              "answer": "No, the extra is $0.54 of tax and $1.27 of tips",
              "explanation": "Gross sales count what the goods earned. Tax and tips pass through the drawer but are not the shop's sales."
            },
            {
              "question": "You want the receipt to end with \"See you tomorrow!\" What do you do?",
              "options": [
                "Change the Receipt footer under Settings as the owner",
                "Ask a developer to edit the receipt code for you",
                "Edit store.json by hand while the app is open",
                "Print the receipts first and write on them"
              ],
              "answer": "Change the Receipt footer under Settings as the owner",
              "explanation": "It is already a setting. Always check Settings and Menu & Catalog before assuming a change needs code, and never edit the file by hand."
            },
            {
              "question": "Why can't you see the .config folder in your file manager by default?",
              "options": [
                "A leading dot in a folder name marks it as hidden",
                "RobOS encrypts the folder while it is not in use",
                "It only exists while a RobOS app is running",
                "It is stored on a separate part of the disk"
              ],
              "answer": "A leading dot in a folder name marks it as hidden",
              "explanation": "On Linux and macOS, names beginning with a dot are hidden from normal views. The folder is an ordinary one, and the instructions above jump straight to it."
            }
          ]
