        {
          "id": "gs-05-backup",
          "title": "Lesson 5: Back up your shop, and prove it works",
          "durationMinutes": 14,
          "overview": "You copy your RobOS files into a folder you choose, then see them come back on a fresh computer. A backup you have never restored is only a hope, so this lesson shows the restore too.",
          "body": "## What Config Sync does\n\n**Config Sync** is a part of the RobOS Preferences app. It copies your RobOS files to a **vault**, which is simply a folder you pick. If that folder is inside Dropbox, OneDrive or Nextcloud, those services carry it off your computer for you. It can also use Google Drive directly, once you sign in under **Cloud Suite Accounts**.\n\n> **Which folder should I pick?** A vault on the same disk as the original protects you from your own mistakes but not from a dead computer. For a real backup, use a folder that syncs somewhere else. A USB drive that you keep elsewhere also works.\n\n## Step 1: Install RobOS Preferences\n\nIn App Manager, search for **RobOS Preferences**, tick it and press **Install selected**. Open it, and **Config Sync & Sharing** is the first item on the left.\n\n## Step 2: Create the vault\n\nOn the **Backup vault** tab, under **Create a new vault**:\n\n1. Set **Store it in** to **A folder on this computer (Dropbox, OneDrive, Nextcloud, NAS…)**.\n2. Set **Parent folder** to the folder that will hold the vault. Type the full path, such as `/home/sam/Dropbox` or `/Users/sam/Dropbox`.\n3. Leave **Folder name** as it is.\n4. Think about **Passphrase**. It encrypts every file in the vault. For a real business, set one, because `store.json` holds your orders and staff list. Write it down: without it, the vault can't be opened.\n5. Press **Create vault**.\n\n> **Known bug in version 0.0.10.** The box suggests `~/Dropbox`, but typing a `~` here puts the vault in a folder literally named `~`, inside the app's own folder, where you would not find it. It is fixed for the next release. Until then, type the full path.\n\n![The Create a new vault form with a folder on this computer selected](/assets/elearning/getting-started/sync-create.png)\n\n## Step 3: Review and apply the first sync\n\nNothing is copied until you agree. RobOS asks how to combine this computer with the vault. Since the vault is empty, keep **Upload this machine to the vault**, which is marked Recommended, and press **Apply**. The button tells you how many changes it will make.\n\n![The first sync screen with Upload this machine to the vault marked as recommended](/assets/elearning/getting-started/sync-plan.png)\n\nYou will see a message like **Synced: 0 file(s) from the vault, 2 to the vault**. Look in your folder and you will find a `RobOS Config Vault` folder holding `robos-config.json`, which lists what is backed up, and a `blobs` folder with the files themselves, compressed. You aren't meant to read those directly.\n\n![The vault marked Connected, showing when it last synced and how many files it tracks](/assets/elearning/getting-started/sync-done.png)\n\n## Step 4: Know when it syncs\n\nDon't assume a backup is happening just because a box is ticked. The **Sync automatically** box says RobOS syncs in the background, but that background job runs as part of **Desktop Manager**. You won't have it if you only installed apps through App Manager, and even if you add it, App Manager doesn't start it when you log in. Only the RobOS desktop does that.\n\n> **Known gap in version 0.0.10.** The box promises background syncing whether or not anything is running it. The next release replaces that promise with the real state: the panel says **Background sync is not running on this computer**, or names the app running it. The next release does not add background syncing to App Manager installs, so the habit below still applies.\n\nSo get into a habit: after a day's work, open this screen and press **Sync now**, then check that **Last sync** updates.\n\n## Step 5: The real test, a new computer\n\nNow imagine your laptop has died. On a new computer, or a new user account on this one:\n\n1. Install App Manager, Point of Sale and RobOS Preferences again.\n2. Make sure your vault folder is there. If you use Dropbox, let Dropbox finish syncing first.\n3. In **Config Sync & Sharing**, go to **Use an existing vault**. Set **Google account** to **— a local folder path —**, and put the full path of the `RobOS Config Vault` folder in **Folder link, ID or path**.\n4. Press **Connect**, keep **Load the vault onto this machine**, and press **Apply**.\n\n![A fresh machine connecting to the vault, with Load the vault onto this machine recommended](/assets/elearning/getting-started/sync-load-vault.png)\n\nOpen Point of Sale. Instead of the welcome screen, you get the PIN pad, because the shop already exists. We ran exactly this on a clean home folder: signing in as the owner showed the same single order, the same $8.16 in cash, and Maple Cold Brew still on the menu.\n\n![The sales report on the new machine, showing the same order and totals as before](/assets/elearning/getting-started/pos-report-restored.png)\n\n> **On Windows,** the **Folder link, ID or path** box in version 0.0.10 only accepts Google Drive links and paths that start with `/` or `~`, so it won't take `C:\\...`. It is fixed for the next release, which accepts drive and network paths such as `C:\\Users\\sam\\Dropbox\\RobOS Config Vault`. Until then, if you are on Windows, use a Google Drive vault instead, and test the restore before you rely on it.\n\n## The trap: a vault is not a time machine\n\nThis matters more than anything else in this lesson. A vault is a **sync**, not a history. It holds the latest version, so a mistake on your computer looks like any other edit.\n\nWe tested it by overwriting the shop's file with garbage, and then pressing **Review changes**. Config Sync offered to upload the damaged file to the vault:\n\n![Review changes offering to send the damaged store.json to the vault](/assets/elearning/getting-started/sync-review-damage.png)\n\nIn version 0.0.10, Config Sync can't tell a damaged file from an edited one. So if something goes wrong:\n\n1. **Stop.** Do not press **Apply** or **Sync now**. Untick **Sync automatically** if you use it.\n2. Press **Disconnect**. This leaves the vault itself alone.\n3. Connect again as in step 5, and this time choose **Load the vault onto this machine**, even though a different option may be marked Recommended.\n\nWe tested that as well, and the good copy came back. It only works if the damage has not been uploaded yet. For anything you can't afford to lose, also keep a dated copy of `store.json` somewhere else from time to time.\n\n> **Coming in the next release.** Config Sync holds back a file that stops being valid JSON, or a larger file that shrinks to under a quarter of its size or goes missing, and asks which version to keep. Background sync stops and notifies you instead of uploading it. A **Load the vault onto this machine** button brings back the vault's copies without disconnecting. Keep your dated copies anyway: damage that still looks like a normal file gets through.\n\n## Restore points\n\nBefore Config Sync changes any file on your computer, it saves the old version as a **restore point**, and it keeps the newest 15. You will find them on the **Restore points** tab, with **Restore all** or **restore this file**. They undo a sync. They are not a general history of your files.\n\n![The Restore points tab listing the files saved before a sync, each with a restore link](/assets/elearning/getting-started/sync-restore-points.png)\n\n## What gets uploaded, and what doesn't\n\nConfig Sync removes sign-ins, tokens, passwords and keys from settings files before anything leaves your computer. It finds them by their names inside JSON settings files, and each computer signs in for itself. Logs, caches and machine identity are skipped as well. That is good protection, but it's not a promise about every file you might ever have, so keep your vault in a private folder and use a passphrase.\n\nIf you did edit the same file on two computers, Config Sync shows it as **Changed on both** and asks whether to **Keep this machine's** or **Take the vault's**. Nothing is overwritten until you choose.\n\n## Sharing, briefly\n\nThe **Share** tab publishes a read-only bundle of chosen settings for someone else, with templates such as **Family & friends** and **New teammate onboarding**. It is separate from your backup. You don't need it today.\n",
          "keyPoints": [
            "A vault is a folder you choose. It is only a real backup if it lives off this computer.",
            "Press Sync now and check Last sync. Background sync needs Desktop Manager.",
            "A vault holds only the latest version, so a mistake can overwrite your good copy.",
            "Prove the backup by restoring it somewhere fresh."
          ],
          "labSteps": [
            "Create a vault and run the first sync.",
            "Make a change and see it waiting to sync.",
            "Optional: restore on a second computer or user account."
          ],
          "labDetails": [
            {
              "description": "Follow steps 1 to 3. If you have no cloud folder, make a new folder in your home folder to practice with, and type its full path.",
              "expected": "A message says the sync finished, the vault is marked **Connected**, and your folder holds `robos-config.json` and `blobs`."
            },
            {
              "description": "In Point of Sale, sell one more drink. Return to Config Sync and press **Review changes**, then **Sync now**.",
              "expected": "Review shows `point-of-sale/store.json` under **To vault**. After syncing, **Last sync** says just now."
            },
            {
              "description": "Follow step 5. A new user account on the same computer is enough for a test, as long as it can read the vault folder.",
              "expected": "Point of Sale opens at the PIN pad, not the welcome screen, and **Reports** shows your sales."
            }
          ],
          "quiz": [
            {
              "question": "You keep your vault in ~/Backups on the same laptop. The laptop is stolen. What do you have?",
              "options": [
                "Nothing, because the vault was on the stolen laptop",
                "Everything, because RobOS also keeps a cloud copy",
                "Only your settings, not your orders or staff list",
                "Everything, because restore points are kept online"
              ],
              "answer": "Nothing, because the vault was on the stolen laptop",
              "explanation": "A vault is only as safe as the folder it lives in. There is no RobOS cloud copy. Use a folder that syncs off the computer."
            },
            {
              "question": "Your shop file is damaged. Review changes offers to send it to the vault. What do you do?",
              "options": [
                "Stop, disconnect, reconnect and load from the vault",
                "Apply it, since the vault keeps every older version",
                "Apply it, then use a restore point to undo the upload",
                "Uninstall the app, which also repairs its damaged data"
              ],
              "answer": "Stop, disconnect, reconnect and load from the vault",
              "explanation": "The vault keeps only the latest version, so uploading would replace your good copy. Restore points only cover changes made to this computer by a sync."
            },
            {
              "question": "Which of these proves your backup works?",
              "options": [
                "Restoring it on a fresh computer and seeing your sales",
                "Seeing a Connected label beside the vault name",
                "Seeing that the blobs folder contains some files",
                "Seeing Sync automatically ticked in the settings"
              ],
              "answer": "Restoring it on a fresh computer and seeing your sales",
              "explanation": "The other three show that something was configured or copied. Only a restore shows that you can get your work back."
            }
          ]
