        {
          "id": "gs-06-ai-safely",
          "title": "Lesson 6: Optional AI, and how to keep it safe",
          "durationMinutes": 10,
          "overview": "Some RobOS apps can use an AI agent you already have. You learn what an AI agent is, how you stay in control, and how RobOS screens what you send. You can skip all of it and lose nothing.",
          "body": "## You can skip this\n\nEverything you have done so far used no AI. If you never set any up, your apps carry on working as normal. This lesson is here so you can decide for yourself.\n\n## What an AI agent is\n\nA chatbot answers questions. An **AI agent** can also do things: read files, run programs and carry out a task with several steps. RobOS doesn't include one or sell one. It works with coding agents you install and sign in to yourself, such as **Claude Code**, **Codex**, **GitHub Copilot** or **Gemini CLI**. Any cost is between you and the company that makes the agent.\n\nTo set one up, install **Agents Manager** from App Manager. It opens as **RobOS Agents**. Each agent in the list has a dot beside it: green means installed and signed in, yellow means installed but signed out, and red means not installed. Choose one and press **Set as Active**.\n\n![RobOS Agents with one agent set as the active provider](/assets/build-apps/shots/agents-2-active.jpg)\n\n## How AI shows up in an app\n\nAI features are marked with a **✦**, and they only run when you press that button. Here's a real example, in Audio Player:\n\n1. Add some music to your library. The **✦ AI Playlist** button is ready once there are tracks.\n2. Press it, and describe the music under **What should it sound like?**, for example *calm songs for opening a coffee shop*.\n3. Press **✦ Generate with AI**. Your agent picks and orders the songs, and you can **Save playlist** or **Play** the result.\n\nThe same app has a **Virtual DJ**, which is off until you turn it on, and talks between songs. Press nothing, and no AI runs.\n\n> **What leaves your computer?** The text of your request and whatever the feature adds to it, such as your song list, go to the company behind the agent you chose. That is the one exception to everything staying local, and it only happens when you press ✦.\n\n## The guard that checks your words\n\nPeople paste the wrong thing into AI tools all the time. So before a quick AI request leaves, RobOS scans it for secrets such as cloud keys and tokens, for personal data such as card numbers, and for **prompt injection**, which is text trying to trick the AI into ignoring its instructions.\n\nWe ran this text, with a made-up key and a standard test card number, through the guard:\n\n```text\nWrite a welcome sign for my coffee shop. Our AWS key is AKIAQ7XKZ2M4TPLN8RWD and my card is 4111 1111 1111 1111.\n```\n\nHere is what the AI would receive:\n\n```text\nWrite a welcome sign for my coffee shop. Our AWS key is [REDACTED:AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID] and my card is [REDACTED:CREDIT_CARD].\n```\n\nYou choose how strict it is in RobOS Preferences, under **Prompt Security & Guardrails**, with **Security Policy Mode**. Press **Save All** after changing it.\n\n![The Prompt Security and Guardrails settings, with Security Policy Mode set to redact](/assets/elearning/getting-started/guard-pane.png)\n\n| Mode | What happens when it finds something |\n|---|---|\n| `redact` (the default) | The sensitive part is masked, and the rest is sent. |\n| `block` | Nothing is sent. |\n| `warn` | It is flagged, and still sent as you typed it unless you use **✦ Auto-Redact** where a box offers it. |\n| `audit-only` | It is sent as typed and noted in the log. |\n| `off` | There are no checks at all. |\n\nStick with `redact`, or use `block` if you would rather fix the text yourself. Each time the guard finds something, it adds an entry to `~/.config/robos/prompt-security-audit.json`. No entry means it found nothing, not that it failed.\n\n## What the guard won't do\n\n- It works by spotting patterns, so it can't catch everything. Your shop's private plans don't match any pattern. **Don't rely on it as your only check.**\n- It screens quick AI requests from apps. A long coding job run by **Task Implementer** does not go through it, so keep secrets out of any project you let an agent work on.\n- It deliberately ignores AWS's famous published example key, which ends in `EXAMPLE`, so don't use that one to test it.\n\n## Using no cloud at all\n\nIf you want no outside company involved, RobOS can answer some quick requests with a model on your own computer through **Ollama**. It's off by default. In RobOS Preferences, open **AI Agents & API Mode**, tick **Use direct model APIs for quick requests (optional)**, set **API provider** to **Ollama (local, no key)**, and press **Install API packages**.\n\nThis doesn't cover everything. **AI Playlist** and **Virtual DJ** use your signed-in agent, so they can't use it, and neither can longer agent tasks.\n",
          "keyPoints": [
            "AI is optional, it runs only when you press ✦, and you choose the agent.",
            "Pressing ✦ sends your request to the company behind that agent.",
            "The guard masks secrets by default, but it only catches patterns."
          ],
          "labSteps": [
            "Check your guard setting.",
            "Optional: see whether you have an agent ready.",
            "Optional: watch the guard catch a fake key."
          ],
          "labDetails": [
            {
              "description": "Open RobOS Preferences and choose **Prompt Security & Guardrails** on the left. Decide which mode suits you, and press **Save All** if you change it.",
              "expected": "**Security Policy Mode** shows your choice. It starts as `redact`."
            },
            {
              "description": "Install **Agents Manager** from App Manager and open it. You don't need to install or buy an agent for this course.",
              "expected": "A list of AI providers, each with a colored dot showing its state."
            },
            {
              "description": "Only if you have an agent set up and Audio Player has music in it: in **✦ AI Playlist**, describe some music and add the made-up key `AKIAQ7XKZ2M4TPLN8RWD`. Press **✦ Generate with AI**, then open the log file named above.",
              "expected": "The log has a new entry naming **AWS Access Key ID**. In `redact` mode the agent never received the key."
            }
          ],
          "quiz": [
            {
              "question": "How do you stop an app from ever using AI?",
              "options": [
                "Do nothing: AI runs only when you press its ✦ button",
                "Disconnect the computer from the internet",
                "Uninstall the separate RobOS AI component",
                "Cancel your RobOS AI subscription plan"
              ],
              "answer": "Do nothing: AI runs only when you press its ✦ button",
              "explanation": "There is no AI component to remove and no subscription. AI features only run when you start them."
            },
            {
              "question": "In redact mode, you paste a prompt holding a real-looking access key. What happens?",
              "options": [
                "The key is masked and the rest of the prompt is sent",
                "The whole prompt is stopped and nothing is sent",
                "The prompt is sent unchanged and noted in a log",
                "The key is encrypted and sent along to the agent"
              ],
              "answer": "The key is masked and the rest of the prompt is sent",
              "explanation": "Masking and sending is what redact means. Stopping is block mode, and sending it unchanged is what audit-only does."
            },
            {
              "question": "Your audit log is empty after a week of using AI features. What does that most likely mean?",
              "options": [
                "The guard has not found anything sensitive to report",
                "The guard is broken and has to be reinstalled",
                "Your prompts have not been reaching your agent",
                "The log only fills up in the audit-only mode"
              ],
              "answer": "The guard has not found anything sensitive to report",
              "explanation": "Entries are only written when something is found. An empty log after clean prompts is what you would expect."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "gs-07-change-the-app",
          "title": "Lesson 7: Changing the app itself, as things stand today",
          "durationMinutes": 12,
          "overview": "Some changes need new code, not a new setting. This lesson is straight about what that takes today: it is a developer task, with an agent doing much of the work. Non-developers get the simple route first.",
          "body": "## First, is it really a code change?\n\nIn lesson 4, you changed a menu, and a receipt footer is just another setting. Look through **Settings** first, because a surprising amount is already there. A code change is for when the app can't do the thing at all, for example: *print the number of items on each receipt*.\n\n## What does not exist yet\n\nYou may have seen an example on the RobOS home page where someone asks Household for a new feature and presses **Plan the change**. That shows where the project wants to go. No app has that button today, and Household in particular is a sandbox with invented data. Changing an app's code currently means working on a copy of its source code, using the steps below.\n\n## Route 1: Ask for it\n\nIf you don't want to touch code, this is your route, and it's a useful one. [Open an issue on GitHub](https://github.com/nddipiazza/robos/issues) or ask on [Discord](https://discord.gg/6PjxzkHujE). A good request says:\n\n- **Who** you are: \"I run a small coffee shop.\"\n- **What** you want: \"Print the number of items on each receipt.\"\n- **Why:** \"My staff check large takeout orders against it.\"\n\n## Route 2: Have an agent change your own copy\n\nThis route suits you if you are comfortable with a terminal, or want to become so. You need [Git](https://git-scm.com/), [Node.js](https://nodejs.org/) and an AI agent that runs on your computer.\n\n### Get a copy and prove it works\n\n```bash\ngit clone https://github.com/nddipiazza/robos.git\ncd robos/packages/point-of-sale\nnpm test\n```\n\n**Git** downloads the code. **Tests** are small programs that check the app still behaves. Run them first, so you know everything worked *before* you changed it. We ran this, and it finished with 67 tests passing and none failing, in about a minute.\n\n### Try it without touching your real shop\n\n```bash\nnode serve.js --port 3411 --data ~/pos-practice\n```\n\nThat runs the app from your copy, and you open it at `http://127.0.0.1:3411` in a browser. **The `--data` part matters.** Leave it out, and this copy uses your real shop's file.\n\n### Ask the agent\n\nStart your agent in the `robos` folder and give it a small, checkable job:\n\n```text\nIn packages/point-of-sale, print the number of items on each receipt, just above the subtotal. Add a test for it. Run npm test and tell me the result. Keep the change as small as you can, and do not commit anything.\n```\n\n### Check its work\n\nAgents make mistakes, and sound confident when they do. Your job is to check:\n\n1. **Read its summary.** Did it change only what you asked? `git status` lists the changed files.\n2. **Run `npm test` yourself** instead of taking its word for it.\n3. **Try it.** Start the app with the command above, sell a drink, and read the receipt.\n4. **Don't like it?** `git restore .` throws away changes to existing files. Your real shop and installed app were never involved.\n\n## How the agent knows its way around\n\nA large project is hard for an agent to understand from code alone. RobOS keeps two things to help:\n\n- **`AGENTS.md`**, a file of instructions and rules for agents, at the top of the repository.\n- **The knowledge graph**, a set of files in the `.robos` folder recording what each app is, along with its documents, tests and how the parts connect.\n\nThey help an agent find the right place, and they are also where this course lives, since the lesson you are reading is stored in that graph. They don't check the agent's work for you. Tests and your own review do that.\n\n## Route 3: Build software with the full RobOS loop\n\nAre you a developer with a project on GitHub? RobOS has a set of apps for planning work and having an agent carry it out:\n\n| Stage | App | What you do |\n|---|---|---|\n| Plan | Task Planner | Turn a description into a list of tasks |\n| Build | Task Implementer | Start an agent on one task, on its own branch |\n| Review | Agent Code Review | Read the changes, and ask for fixes |\n| Ship | GitHub | Merge the pull request |\n\n![Task Implementer after an agent has finished a task on its own branch](/assets/build-apps/shots/implementer-2-done.jpg)\n\nThe [Build apps guide](https://rowbose.com/build-apps/solo/) walks through it with a sample project. It needs a signed-in GitHub CLI, a clone of your repository and an agent. Be aware that Task Implementer runs the agent as you, with permission prompts turned off and no sandbox, so keep it to projects where that is acceptable.\n",
          "keyPoints": [
            "Check Settings first. Many changes do not need code at all.",
            "No app has a built-in \"change this app\" button today. Ask for it, or work on a copy of the source.",
            "Run the tests before and after. Your own checks are what make an agent's work safe to keep."
          ],
          "labSteps": [
            "Write your change request.",
            "Optional: get the code and run the tests.",
            "Optional: run your copy without touching your real shop."
          ],
          "labDetails": [
            {
              "description": "Think of one thing you would change in an app you tried. First check whether a setting already does it. Then write it using who, what and why.",
              "expected": "Three short sentences that a stranger could act on. Post them as a GitHub issue if you would like it to happen."
            },
            {
              "description": "You need Git and Node.js installed. Run these from a terminal.",
              "code": "git clone https://github.com/nddipiazza/robos.git\ncd robos/packages/point-of-sale\nnpm test",
              "expected": "A summary at the end with `fail 0`. The download is large and the tests take around a minute."
            },
            {
              "description": "From the same folder, start the app with its own practice data. Press `Ctrl+C` in the terminal to stop it when you finish.",
              "code": "node serve.js --port 3411 --data ~/pos-practice",
              "expected": "The terminal prints `RobOS Point of Sale on http://127.0.0.1:3411`. Opening that address shows the welcome screen, not your shop, which proves your real data is untouched."
            }
          ],
          "quiz": [
            {
              "question": "You want your shop's name on the receipt. What should you try first?",
              "options": [
                "Look under Settings, since it is probably already there",
                "Clone the code and ask an agent to add it for you",
                "Open a GitHub issue and wait for the next release",
                "Open store.json and type the name in by hand"
              ],
              "answer": "Look under Settings, since it is probably already there",
              "explanation": "Business name and receipt footer are settings. Check the app before you reach for code."
            },
            {
              "question": "You run the practice command but leave out --data. What is the risk?",
              "options": [
                "The copy uses your real shop's file, and may change it",
                "The app will not start without a data folder",
                "The app uploads your sales to a public server",
                "Your installed copy of the app gets uninstalled"
              ],
              "answer": "The copy uses your real shop's file, and may change it",
              "explanation": "By default the server uses ~/.config/robos/point-of-sale, the same file your installed app uses."
            },
            {
              "question": "An agent says \"all tests pass, the feature works\". What should you do?",
              "options": [
                "Run the tests yourself and try the feature by hand",
                "Accept it, because agents cannot report false results",
                "Ask the agent again whether it is completely sure",
                "Check that the knowledge graph has been updated"
              ],
              "answer": "Run the tests yourself and try the feature by hand",
              "explanation": "A report is only a claim. Running the tests and using the feature yourself is the check that counts."
            }
