    {
      "@id": "urn:robos:elearning:course:how-to-recreate-classic-jrpg-using-kgraph-parsing-with-automated-playthrough",
      "@type": [
        "oslc_am:Resource",
        "robos:ELearningCourse",
        "robos:ELearning",
        "schema:Course"
      ],
      "dcterms:title": "How to Rebuild a Classic Video Game",
      "dcterms:description": "A from-zero, plain-English course. Follow a tiny made-up game through every stage, with real screen recordings: collect a fan site, pull out the facts, connect them into a knowledge graph, rebuild a playable game, and have a robot play it. Then see what the same steps produced for the real Final Fantasy on the NES, and learn game programming and Godot using a complete game. Works with any AI agent.",
      "robos:topic": "Scraping, Parsing, Knowledge Graphs, Automated Playthroughs and Godot, from Zero",
      "robos:difficulty": "Beginner",
      "robos:level": "Beginner",
      "robos:targetAudience": "Curious beginners and game fans. No programming needed to follow along.",
      "robos:estimatedDuration": "265 minutes",
      "robos:gitopsFile": ".robos/elearning.yaml",
      "robos:targetApplication": "urn:robos:library:robos-jrpg-parser",
      "robos:documentationPage": "docs/elearning/how-to-recreate-classic-jrpg-using-kgraph-parsing-with-automated-playthrough.md",
      "robos:webUrl": "/elearning/how-to-recreate-classic-jrpg-using-kgraph-parsing-with-automated-playthrough/",
      "robos:status": "published",
      "robos:featured": true,
      "robos:modulesCount": 12,
      "robos:package": "learning",
      "robos:namespace": "robos.learning",
      "robos:schemaOrgType": "https://schema.org/Course",
      "robos:domainStandard": "https://schema.org/Course",
      "robos:updatedAt": "2026-11-04T21:00:00.000Z",
      "robos:modules": [
        {
          "id": "mod-01-start-here",
          "title": "Module 1: Start Here. What Are We Actually Doing?",
          "durationMinutes": 10,
          "overview": "The whole project in plain English, before any technical words: what we are building, the five stages, and the tiny made-up game that will be our example all the way through.",
          "lessonHtml": "<div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">The idea in one paragraph</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">Imagine a much-loved board game that went out of print decades ago. You can't buy it, but fans have written about it for years: photos of the board, lists of every card, advice on how to win. If you gathered all of that and read it carefully, you could rebuild the game.</p><p class=\"lesson-text\">This course does the same thing with an old video game. A computer helps us <strong>collect</strong> what fans wrote, <strong>read</strong> it, <strong>organize</strong> it into a tidy set of facts, <strong>rebuild</strong> the game from those facts, and then <strong>check</strong> the result by having a robot play it from start to finish.</p><div class=\"callout\"><strong>You do not need to be a programmer</strong>You can follow the whole course by reading it and watching the clips. The hands-on labs are optional, and each one tells you exactly what to type and what you should see.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">The five stages</h4><div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;margin:14px 0;align-items:stretch;\"><div style=\"flex:1 1 150px;background:var(--bg-card);border:1px solid var(--border);border-top:3px solid var(--accent);border-radius:8px;padding:10px 12px;\"><div style=\"font-size:12px;color:var(--accent-cyan);font-weight:700;\">Stage 1</div><div style=\"font-weight:700;color:var(--text-bright);margin:2px 0 4px;\">Collect</div><div style=\"font-size:12.5px;line-height:1.5;\">Gather maps and guide pages into one folder. Copying them with a program is called <em>scraping</em>.</div></div><div style=\"flex:1 1 150px;background:var(--bg-card);border:1px solid var(--border);border-top:3px solid var(--accent);border-radius:8px;padding:10px 12px;\"><div style=\"font-size:12px;color:var(--accent-cyan);font-weight:700;\">Stage 2</div><div style=\"font-weight:700;color:var(--text-bright);margin:2px 0 4px;\">Read</div><div style=\"font-size:12.5px;line-height:1.5;\">A program called a <em>parser</em> pulls facts out of those messy pages.</div></div><div style=\"flex:1 1 150px;background:var(--bg-card);border:1px solid var(--border);border-top:3px solid var(--accent);border-radius:8px;padding:10px 12px;\"><div style=\"font-size:12px;color:var(--accent-cyan);font-weight:700;\">Stage 3</div><div style=\"font-weight:700;color:var(--text-bright);margin:2px 0 4px;\">Organize</div><div style=\"font-size:12.5px;line-height:1.5;\">The facts go into a <em>knowledge graph</em>: every fact connected to the things it relates to.</div></div><div style=\"flex:1 1 150px;background:var(--bg-card);border:1px solid var(--border);border-top:3px solid var(--accent);border-radius:8px;padding:10px 12px;\"><div style=\"font-size:12px;color:var(--accent-cyan);font-weight:700;\">Stage 4</div><div style=\"font-weight:700;color:var(--text-bright);margin:2px 0 4px;\">Rebuild</div><div style=\"font-size:12.5px;line-height:1.5;\">Turn the facts into maps, shops and battles you can actually play.</div></div><div style=\"flex:1 1 150px;background:var(--bg-card);border:1px solid var(--border);border-top:3px solid var(--accent);border-radius:8px;padding:10px 12px;\"><div style=\"font-size:12px;color:var(--accent-cyan);font-weight:700;\">Stage 5</div><div style=\"font-weight:700;color:var(--text-bright);margin:2px 0 4px;\">Check</div><div style=\"font-size:12.5px;line-height:1.5;\">A robot plays the game like a person would and reports anything that's wrong.</div></div></div><p class=\"lesson-text\">That is the entire course. Every module that follows is one of these stages, explained slowly, with something real to look at.</p></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Our example: Ember Quest</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">A real game is too big to hold in your head while you are learning, and examples quickly turn abstract. So we made up a game small enough to see all of it at once. <strong>Ember Quest does not exist.</strong> We invented its town, its cave, its one boss and its fan website for this course.</p><div style=\"overflow-x:auto;margin:12px 0;\"><table style=\"border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.5;\"><thead><tr><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">In Ember Quest</th><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">Size</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Places you can walk around</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">2 (Ashford Town and Emberfall Cave)</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Weapons for sale</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">3</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Monsters</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">2, plus 1 boss</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Fan website pages to collect</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">7 guide pages and 3 maps</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"lesson-text\">Everything you see in this course's clips really ran: the real download tool, the real parser, and a real game built from their output. Using an invented game means we own every pixel, so we can show you all of it.</p><div class=\"doc-figure\"><img src=\"/assets/elearning/jrpg-kgraph/demo/robot-in-the-shop.png\" alt=\"Ember Quest running in a browser. The hero stands at the shop door, and the robot's script is ticking off steps beside the game.\" class=\"doc-img\" loading=\"lazy\" onclick=\"openLightbox(this.src, this.alt)\"><div class=\"doc-caption\">Where we end up: Ember Quest, built from a fan site's tables, being played by a robot.</div></div></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">And then the real thing</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">Once you understand the small version, Module 7 shows what the same stages produced for a real game, the 1990 American release of <em>Final Fantasy</em> for the Nintendo Entertainment System: 1,206 connected facts, and a playable version a robot could fight its way through.</p></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">How you will do the hands-on parts</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">You talk to an <strong>AI agent</strong>, and it does the typing. An AI agent is an assistant such as Claude Code, GitHub Copilot, Codex or Gemini that can read a project's files and run programs on your computer. You describe what you want in plain English, and it finds the right program, runs it and tells you what happened.</p><p class=\"lesson-text\">So every hands-on step in this course gives you a sentence to paste into your agent, an explanation of what that will do, and the exact command in case you'd rather type it yourself.</p><div class=\"callout\"><strong>We use Claude as the example, but any agent works</strong>The examples were tested with Claude Code. They are ordinary sentences with nothing specific to Claude in them, so any agent that can read files and run commands should handle them. Its replies will just be worded differently.</div></div>",
          "labSteps": [
            "Name the five stages from memory",
            "Spot each stage in the finished example"
          ],
          "labDetails": [
            {
              "title": "Name the five stages from memory",
              "who": "You. No computer needed.",
              "description": "Look away from this page and say the five stages in order, in your own words. A good version sounds like explaining it to a friend over coffee.",
              "expected": "Collect, read, organize, rebuild, check. If you said \"copy the fan pages, pull out the facts, connect them up, make the game, let a robot test it\", that is exactly right."
            },
            {
              "title": "Spot each stage in the finished example",
              "who": "You. No computer needed.",
              "description": "Look at the picture below. The town map came from the Collect stage. The price on the sword came from Read and Organize. The game itself is Rebuild. The green ticks are Check.",
              "expected": "You can point at something in the picture for each stage. The next modules explain how each one got there.",
              "image": "/assets/elearning/jrpg-kgraph/demo/robot-in-the-shop.png",
              "imageAlt": "Ember Quest with its shop menu open, next to the robot's checklist"
            }
          ],
          "quiz": [
            {
              "question": "Which description best matches what this course teaches you to do?",
              "options": [
                "Rebuild an old game by collecting what fans wrote, organizing it into facts, and checking it with a robot",
                "Copy an old game's disc onto a modern computer so that it runs exactly as it did in 1990",
                "Ask an AI model to invent a brand new game that merely looks similar to an old classic one",
                "Download a finished remake that someone else already made and change the colors on its menus"
              ],
              "answer": "Rebuild an old game by collecting what fans wrote, organizing it into facts, and checking it with a robot",
              "explanation": "The project works from descriptions of the game. It gathers them, turns them into organized facts, builds from those facts, and verifies the result by playing it."
            },
            {
              "question": "Why does this course use a made-up game called Ember Quest for its examples?",
              "options": [
                "Because the real tools are not able to read anything about a commercially released video game",
                "Because it is small enough to see completely, and we own all of it so we can show every part",
                "Because a real game would have to be bought first, and the course wants to stay completely free",
                "Because Ember Quest is a famous 1989 classic that many readers will already remember playing"
              ],
              "answer": "Because it is small enough to see completely, and we own all of it so we can show every part",
              "explanation": "A tiny invented game keeps examples concrete and lets the course publish every map and page. Real game material stays private because we do not have the rights to it."
            },
            {
              "question": "What does the robot do in the final stage of the project?",
              "options": [
                "It scans the internet looking for any fan websites that have not been collected into the folder",
                "It writes new story chapters for the game whenever the original guide pages run out of material",
                "It plays the rebuilt game by pressing keys and checks that what happens matches what should happen",
                "It paints new artwork for each town using the colors that it finds in the collected map pictures"
              ],
              "answer": "It plays the rebuilt game by pressing keys and checks that what happens matches what should happen",
              "explanation": "The robot is a tester. It presses the same keys a person would and compares the game's state against a written list of expectations."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "mod-02-before-you-begin",
          "title": "Module 2: Before You Begin. The Words and Ideas You Will Need",
          "durationMinutes": 25,
          "overview": "A from-zero primer. What makes up a role-playing game, how computers store the things we will collect, what the long words in this course's title mean, the rules we follow, and how to set up your computer if you want to do the labs.",
          "lessonHtml": "<div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Part 1: What is inside an old role-playing game?</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">A <strong>JRPG</strong> (Japanese role-playing game) is a story-driven game where you walk a small party of heroes around a world, buy equipment, and fight monsters in turns. It sounds rich, but underneath, it's surprisingly simple. Almost everything is a list of numbers plus a few rules.</p><div style=\"overflow-x:auto;margin:12px 0;\"><table style=\"border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.5;\"><thead><tr><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">Word</th><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">Plain meaning</th><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">In Ember Quest</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">HP (hit points)</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">How much damage something can take before it is defeated.</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">The Mossling has 12 HP. The Cinder Wyrm has 40.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Stats</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">The numbers that describe a character, monster or item.</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">The Iron Sword does 8 damage and costs 40 gold.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Class</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A type of hero, which decides what they can use.</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Only the Warden can use an Iron Sword.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Tile</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">One small square of a map, like a single floor tile in a bathroom.</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Every map is 16 tiles wide and 12 tall. Each tile is 16 pixels square.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Sprite</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A small picture of a character or thing that moves around.</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">The little hero in the red cap.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Boss</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A tough, one-off enemy that usually ends a chapter.</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">The Cinder Wyrm, waiting on the altar.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Walkthrough</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A fan-written guide that explains how to finish the game.</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">The site's Walkthrough page.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout\"><strong>Why old games work well for this</strong>The Nintendo Entertainment System (NES), sold from the mid-1980s, had very little memory. Its games had to be built from tiny repeating tiles and short tables of numbers. That simplicity is what makes it possible for fans to document a whole game, and for us to rebuild one.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Part 2: How computers store what we collect</h4><div style=\"overflow-x:auto;margin:12px 0;\"><table style=\"border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.5;\"><thead><tr><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">Word</th><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">Plain meaning</th><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">An everyday comparison</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">File and folder</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A file is one saved thing. A folder holds files.</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A document and a filing cabinet drawer.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Web page (HTML)</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A file of text with labels saying \"this is a heading\" or \"this is a table\". Your browser turns it into what you see.</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A typed page with instructions for how to lay it out.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Link</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A part of a web page that points to another address.</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A page reference: \"see page 40\".</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">PNG</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A common format for picture files.</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A photo print, but made of exact tiny squares.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Program or script</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A list of instructions a computer follows.</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A recipe.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Terminal</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A window where you type instructions as text instead of clicking.</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Texting your computer.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Command</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">One instruction you type in the terminal.</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">One text message.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">localhost (127.0.0.1)</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">An address meaning \"this computer\". A website here is only visible to you.</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Talking to yourself in your own kitchen.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"lesson-text\">One more format matters a lot, because the rest of the course leans on it. <strong>JSON</strong> is a way of writing down facts that both people and programs can read. Here are the main lines of the entry our parser produced for the Iron Sword:</p><pre class=\"code-snippet\"><code>{\n  \"name\": \"Iron Sword\",\n  \"kind\": \"weapon\",\n  \"damage\": 8,\n  \"price\": 40,\n  \"source\": \"guide/weapons.html\"\n}</code></pre><p class=\"lesson-text\">Each line is a label and a value. That's all it is. Notice the last line: every fact remembers <strong>where it came from</strong>. This idea will save us more than once.</p></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Part 3: The words in this course's title</h4><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Scraping</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\"><strong>Scraping</strong> means having a program visit web pages and save a copy of what is there, instead of a person clicking \"Save\" over and over. The program that does this here is called the <strong>harvester</strong>.</p></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Parsing</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\"><strong>Parsing</strong> means reading messy material and pulling out structured facts. You do it yourself when you read a restaurant menu and note down \"soup, $6\". Our <strong>parser</strong> does it to guide pages.</p></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Knowledge graph (kgraph)</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">A <strong>knowledge graph</strong> is a set of facts stored as a web of connections rather than a pile of separate lists. Think of a family tree, or a detective's corkboard with string running between photos. Each item is a <strong>node</strong>, and each string between them is a <strong>link</strong>.</p><svg class=\"graph-figure\" viewBox=\"0 0 680 280\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"A small knowledge graph with Iron Sword in the middle, linked to its price, damage, the class that can use it and the guide page it came from\" style=\"max-width:100%;display:block;margin:12px auto;\">\n<g class=\"edge\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"><line x1=\"340\" y1=\"118\" x2=\"150\" y2=\"62\"/><line x1=\"340\" y1=\"118\" x2=\"530\" y2=\"62\"/><line x1=\"340\" y1=\"162\" x2=\"150\" y2=\"218\"/><line x1=\"340\" y1=\"162\" x2=\"530\" y2=\"218\"/></g>\n<g font-size=\"12\" text-anchor=\"middle\"><text x=\"225\" y=\"78\">costs</text><text x=\"455\" y=\"78\">does damage</text><text x=\"225\" y=\"205\">can be used by</text><text x=\"455\" y=\"205\">the fact came from</text></g>\n<g font-size=\"13\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\">\n<rect class=\"node-main\" x=\"262\" y=\"118\" width=\"156\" height=\"44\" rx=\"8\"/><text class=\"node-main-label\" x=\"340\" y=\"145\">Iron Sword</text>\n<rect class=\"node\" x=\"60\" y=\"38\" width=\"110\" height=\"38\" rx=\"8\"/><text x=\"115\" y=\"62\">40 gold</text>\n<rect class=\"node\" x=\"510\" y=\"38\" width=\"110\" height=\"38\" rx=\"8\"/><text x=\"565\" y=\"62\">8 points</text>\n<rect class=\"node\" x=\"60\" y=\"204\" width=\"110\" height=\"38\" rx=\"8\"/><text x=\"115\" y=\"228\">Warden class</text>\n<rect class=\"node\" x=\"490\" y=\"204\" width=\"150\" height=\"38\" rx=\"8\"/><text x=\"565\" y=\"228\">The weapons page</text>\n</g></svg><p class=\"lesson-text\">Once facts are connected, a program can answer questions a flat list can't, such as \"which weapons can a Warden afford in the first town, and can we prove where each price came from?\"</p></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Automated playthrough</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">A <strong>playthrough</strong> is playing a game from start to finish. <strong>Automated</strong> means a robot does it. The robot follows a script written in near-normal English using a format called <strong>Gherkin</strong>, run by a tool called <strong>Cucumber</strong>. Scripts read like this:</p><pre class=\"code-snippet\"><code>When the hero walks to the weapon shop\nAnd the hero buys the \"Iron Sword\"\nThen the hero has 10 gold</code></pre><p class=\"lesson-text\">If the hero doesn't end up with exactly 10 gold, the test fails and tells you so.</p></div></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Part 4: A few words you'll meet later</h4><div style=\"overflow-x:auto;margin:12px 0;\"><table style=\"border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.5;\"><thead><tr><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">Word</th><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">Plain meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Game engine (Godot)</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Ready-made software for building games, handling drawing, sound and input. Godot is a free one. We used it for the real Final Fantasy build.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">ROM</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A copy of the data on an old game cartridge.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Disassembly</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A game's internal instructions, turned back into a form people can read and annotate.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Clean-room art</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">New artwork we generate ourselves instead of reusing the original game's pictures.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">AI agent</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A language model given a specific job. Here it reads long passages of guide text and reports what it finds.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Part 5: The ground rules</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">Fans wrote those guides, and companies own those games. Being able to copy something doesn't make it yours. We hold ourselves to these rules. They are our project's practice rather than legal advice, so check what applies to you.</p><ul style=\"margin:0 0 12px 20px;font-size:14px;line-height:1.7;\"><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\"><strong>People collect from real fan sites by hand.</strong> We do not point automated downloaders at real fan sites. The harvester enforces this itself: it refuses any address outside your own computer, so in this course it only ever talks to the practice site.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\"><strong>What you collect stays private.</strong> Guide pages, map pictures and game data live in a private folder. They are never uploaded or published.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\"><strong>We only publish what we made ourselves:</strong> the organized facts and newly generated art, with source names and quotes removed. A special <code>publish</code> command makes that clean copy (see Module 7).</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\"><strong>If you ever write your own scraper for a site that gave you permission,</strong> read its rules first, say honestly who you are, go slowly, and stop if you are asked to.</li></ul><div class=\"callout warn\"><strong>Why you don't see any real fan site in this course</strong>A screen recording of someone's website is a copy of that website. Publishing one here would break our second rule. That's why every clip shows our own practice site, which behaves the same way and belongs to us.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Part 6: Working with an AI agent</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">An AI agent is a chat assistant that can also act: it can read the files in a project and run programs on your computer. This is different from a chat website, which can only talk.</p><div style=\"overflow-x:auto;margin:12px 0;\"><table style=\"border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.5;\"><thead><tr><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">Agents you might use</th><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">Where it runs</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Claude Code</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A terminal or a desktop app</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">GitHub Copilot</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Visual Studio Code, other editors or a terminal, using its agent mode</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Codex</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A terminal or an editor</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Gemini CLI</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A terminal</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Others, such as Cursor</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Any agent works if it can read files and run commands</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"lesson-text\">Here is what a conversation looks like. This is an illustration, and your agent's wording will differ:</p><div style=\"overflow-x:auto;margin:12px 0;\"><table style=\"border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.5;\"><thead><tr><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">Who</th><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">What they say or do</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\"><strong>You</strong></td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Run the course's check for the Ember Quest demo and tell me if it passed.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\"><strong>Agent</strong></td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Reads the project's instructions, then asks: \"May I run <code>node --test packages/robos-test/tests/jrpg-parser/practice-site.test.js</code>?\"</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\"><strong>You</strong></td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Read it and approve.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\"><strong>Agent</strong></td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">\"It passed: 6 tests passed and none failed.\"</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout\"><strong>Stay in charge</strong>Your agent can change files, so read what it asks to run. If you don't understand a request, ask \"what will that do?\" before you approve it. Each step in this course says what should happen, so you can tell when something looks off. Agents also make mistakes, so check their claims against the \"What to expect\" notes.</div><p class=\"lesson-text\">The project includes instruction files that agents read automatically, such as <code>AGENTS.md</code>, <code>.github/copilot-instructions.md</code> and <code>GEMINI.md</code>. That's how a short sentence from you can turn into the right command.</p></div>",
          "labSteps": [
            "Open an AI agent",
            "Download the project",
            "Check the tools the labs need",
            "Prove everything works"
          ],
          "labDetails": [
            {
              "title": "Open an AI agent",
              "who": "You",
              "description": "Install any agent you like by following its official instructions, and open it. It will usually ask you to sign in. If you already use one, you're ready. Then ask it the question below to make sure it can do things rather than only chat.",
              "agentPrompt": "Which folder are you working in, and can you run commands on my computer?",
              "explain": "Nothing changes. This just confirms that your agent can see your computer and run programs.",
              "expected": "It names a folder and says it can run commands, often asking your permission first. If it says it can only chat, you are in a chat-only mode. Look for its agent mode, or try a different agent."
            },
            {
              "title": "Download the project",
              "who": "Your AI agent",
              "description": "This copies the whole RobOS project, including all the programs in this course, to your computer.",
              "agentPrompt": "Clone https://github.com/nddipiazza/robos.git into a folder called robos in my home folder, and tell me when it has finished.",
              "explain": "`git clone` downloads a copy of a project into a new folder. It's a big project, so it can take a few minutes. It changes nothing except creating that folder.",
              "code": "cd ~\ngit clone https://github.com/nddipiazza/robos.git",
              "expected": "Your agent reports the clone finished. **Then restart your agent inside the new `robos` folder**, because every later step assumes it's working there."
            },
            {
              "title": "Check the tools the labs need",
              "who": "Your AI agent",
              "description": "The programs in this course are run by Node.js and Python, so both need to be installed.",
              "agentPrompt": "Check whether git, Node.js and Python 3 are installed and tell me their versions. If one is missing, tell me how to install it, and ask me before installing anything.",
              "explain": "Each tool is asked to print its version number. That is all, so nothing changes.",
              "code": "git --version\nnode --version\npython3 --version",
              "expected": "Three version numbers. You want Node 18 or newer and Python 3.8 or newer."
            },
            {
              "title": "Prove everything works",
              "who": "Your AI agent",
              "description": "The project has a test that runs this whole course's example from start to finish, in a temporary folder that it deletes afterwards.",
              "agentPrompt": "Run the check for the Ember Quest demo, described in packages/robos-jrpg-parser/demo/README.md, and tell me how many tests passed.",
              "explain": "It builds the practice website, runs the harvester and parser on it, and has a robot play the resulting game. It all happens on your computer, and takes a few seconds.",
              "code": "node --test packages/robos-test/tests/jrpg-parser/practice-site.test.js",
              "expected": "6 tests pass and none fail. If so, your computer can do every step up to Module 7."
            }
          ],
          "quiz": [
            {
              "question": "What does it mean to say that a program is \"parsing\" a guide page?",
              "options": [
                "It is reading the messy page and pulling out structured facts such as names, prices and damage",
                "It is making a perfect backup copy of the page so that the page cannot ever be lost or changed",
                "It is uploading the page to a public website so that other players are able to read it as well",
                "It is checking whether the person who wrote the page has given permission for it to be copied"
              ],
              "answer": "It is reading the messy page and pulling out structured facts such as names, prices and damage",
              "explanation": "Parsing turns material written for people into structured facts for programs, just as you might read a menu and write down \"soup, $6\"."
            },
            {
              "question": "What makes a knowledge graph different from a simple list of items?",
              "options": [
                "A knowledge graph can only be stored online, whereas a simple list is kept on your own computer",
                "A knowledge graph connects each fact to related things, so you can follow the links and ask questions",
                "A knowledge graph always holds much more text than a list, because every item includes a full essay",
                "A knowledge graph sorts its items alphabetically, whereas a simple list keeps them in random order"
              ],
              "answer": "A knowledge graph connects each fact to related things, so you can follow the links and ask questions",
              "explanation": "The links are the point. Because the Iron Sword connects to its price, its class and its source page, a program can follow those strings to answer connected questions."
            },
            {
              "question": "Why does every clip in this course show a practice website rather than a real fan site?",
              "options": [
                "Real fan sites have been shut down, so there are no more of them left that anyone could record",
                "The harvester program is unable to read pages unless they are stored on the very same computer",
                "A recording of a site copies it, and we only publish material that we made and have the right to share",
                "A practice site loads quite a lot faster, which makes the recorded video clips look much smoother"
              ],
              "answer": "A recording of a site copies it, and we only publish material that we made and have the right to share",
              "explanation": "A screen recording of someone else's site would republish their work. Using our own invented site means every pixel is ours to show."
            },
            {
              "question": "What is the main difference between an AI agent and an ordinary chat website?",
              "options": [
                "An AI agent can read a project's files and run programs on your computer, usually asking your permission first",
                "An AI agent never makes any mistakes, so there is no need to read or check anything that it wants to do",
                "An AI agent only works with the Claude model, whereas the chat websites can use any model that you choose",
                "An AI agent works entirely offline, so it does not need any kind of account or subscription or connection"
              ],
              "answer": "An AI agent can read a project's files and run programs on your computer, usually asking your permission first",
              "explanation": "That ability to act is what makes an agent useful here, and also why you should read what it asks to run. Claude Code, Copilot, Codex and others all work this way."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "mod-03-collecting",
          "title": "Module 3: Stage 1, Collecting. What Scraping Actually Looks Like",
          "durationMinutes": 20,
          "overview": "See what scraping really is, and exactly who does what. In this course, your AI agent runs a program that collects files from a practice website on your own computer. On a real game, you collect them by hand.",
          "lessonHtml": "<div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">First: what are you actually being asked to do?</h4><div class=\"callout\"><strong>Short answer</strong>In this course, <strong>you do not go to any website or download anything by hand.</strong> You ask your AI agent to run a program, and that program downloads the files from a practice website on your own computer.</div><p class=\"lesson-text\">There are really two different situations, and it's easy to mix them up:</p><div style=\"overflow-x:auto;margin:12px 0;\"><table style=\"border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.5;\"><thead><tr><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\"></th><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">In this course (Ember Quest)</th><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">On a real game (later, on your own)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\"><strong>Where the files come from</strong></td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A made-up website running on your own computer</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Real fan websites on the internet</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\"><strong>Who collects them</strong></td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A program called the harvester, which your agent runs</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\"><strong>You, by hand</strong></td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\"><strong>What you do</strong></td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Paste a sentence into your agent</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Open each page in your browser, and use \"Save Page As\" or right-click a map and \"Save Image As\"</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\"><strong>Where they end up</strong></td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A folder on your computer</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A private folder on your computer</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\"><strong>Is it automated?</strong></td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Yes, because the site is ours</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">No. We don't point automated tools at real fan sites, and the harvester refuses to connect to them</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"lesson-text\">So why show a scraper at all? Because \"scraping\" is a term you will hear constantly, and you should see exactly what it means. It also gets the files into the folder, so you can do every later step.</p><p class=\"lesson-text\">Here is who does each step in this module:</p><div style=\"overflow-x:auto;margin:12px 0;\"><table style=\"border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.5;\"><thead><tr><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">Step</th><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">Who does it</th><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">What happens</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">1. Create the practice website</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A program, run by your agent</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A folder fills with web pages and map pictures</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">2. Start it</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A program, run by your agent</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Your computer starts acting as a small website</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">3. Look at it (optional)</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">You, in your browser</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">You see what the program will be reading</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">4. Make the shopping list</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">The harvester, run by your agent</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A list of files to fetch, with nothing downloaded yet</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">5. Add the guide pages to the list</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A helper program, run by your agent</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">The list grows from 3 files to 10</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">6. Download everything</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">The harvester, run by your agent</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">10 files arrive in your folder</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">What we need, and why one page is not enough</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">Think about what it would take to rebuild a game. A walkthrough tells you the story and where to go. It won't tell you where every wall stands or how hard a monster hits. So we gather different kinds of material:</p><div style=\"overflow-x:auto;margin:12px 0;\"><table style=\"border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.5;\"><thead><tr><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">We collect</th><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">Which gives us</th><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">In Ember Quest</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Map pictures</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">What each place looks like, tile by tile</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">3 PNG files</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Weapon and armor tables</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Prices and strengths</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">The Weapons page</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Monster charts</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">HP, attack, rewards</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">The Monsters page</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Treasure lists</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">What is in each chest, and where</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">The Treasure page</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Walkthrough</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">The order of events</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">The Walkthrough page</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout\"><strong>Meet the practice site</strong>The clips below use \"The Ember Quest Archive\", a made-up fan site running on one computer. Its address, <code>127.0.0.1:8765</code>, means \"this machine\". Nothing in these clips touches the internet. <strong>The terminal panel on the right shows the commands your agent would run for you.</strong> You don't need to type them.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Watch it: finding what to download</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">Real sites are messy. Our atlas page lists maps for two games, and we only want one. So before downloading anything, a program called the <strong>harvester</strong> reads the page and writes a <strong>manifest</strong>, which is just a shopping list of files.</p><div class=\"doc-figure\"><img src=\"/assets/elearning/jrpg-kgraph/demo/scrape-discover.gif\" alt=\"Screen recording: a browser opens the Ember Quest Archive, clicks through to the map atlas, marks three Ember Quest maps to keep and two Star Knight maps to ignore, then the harvester writes a list of the three files.\" class=\"doc-img\" loading=\"lazy\" onclick=\"openLightbox(this.src, this.alt)\"><div class=\"doc-caption\">A real recording of an embedded browser and the real harvester program, running together. Click to enlarge.</div></div><details style=\"margin:-10px 0 18px;font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.6;\"><summary style=\"cursor:pointer;color:var(--accent-cyan);\">Read what happens in this clip</summary><ol style=\"margin:0 0 12px 20px;font-size:14px;line-height:1.7;\"><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\">You can look at the practice site in a browser if you want to, but you won't be downloading anything by hand. The panel at the bottom right is the website's own log: every line is something it was asked for.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\">The map atlas lists maps for two different games. A program is about to read this same page.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\">The rule is simple: keep files whose names start with <code>EmberQuest-</code>. Green rows match it, red rows don't.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\">Your agent runs the harvester. It fetches that one page, finds the links, and applies the same rule. It downloads nothing yet.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\">The shopping list holds exactly three maps, with nothing from the other game.</li></ol></details></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">The command, explained word by word</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">You don't have to type this, because your agent will. But you should understand what it's asking to run before you approve it, and each part is just a piece of a sentence:</p><pre class=\"code-snippet\"><code>python3 packages/robos-jrpg-parser/tools/harvest_jrpg_assets.py \\\n  --out-dir ~/ember-quest/collection \\\n  --vgmaps-index http://127.0.0.1:8765/atlas/ \\\n  --base-url http://127.0.0.1:8765/atlas/ \\\n  --map-prefix EmberQuest- \\\n  --discover-only</code></pre><div style=\"overflow-x:auto;margin:12px 0;\"><table style=\"border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.5;\"><thead><tr><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">This part</th><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">Means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\"><code>python3 ...harvest_jrpg_assets.py</code></td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Run the harvester program.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\"><code>--out-dir</code></td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Put everything in this folder.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\"><code>--vgmaps-index</code></td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">This is the page that lists the maps.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\"><code>--base-url</code></td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Links on that page are relative to this address.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\"><code>--map-prefix EmberQuest-</code></td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Only keep files whose names start like this.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\"><code>--discover-only</code></td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Just write the list. Do not download yet.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"lesson-text\">The harvester also has a built-in limit: it refuses any address outside your own computer, including links and redirects that try to lead somewhere else. Real websites are collected by a person, by hand.</p></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Watch it: downloading everything</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">The harvester can find maps by itself, but it has no way to know which guide pages matter. So the demo includes a small helper program, <code>collect.js</code>, which adds our seven guide pages to the list. Then the harvester works through the whole list.</p><div class=\"doc-figure\"><img src=\"/assets/elearning/jrpg-kgraph/demo/scrape-download.gif\" alt=\"Screen recording: the harvester downloads ten files one at a time, with a line printed for each, while the collection folder listing fills up from empty to ten files.\" class=\"doc-img\" loading=\"lazy\" onclick=\"openLightbox(this.src, this.alt)\"><div class=\"doc-caption\">The folder at the bottom right starts empty and fills up as each file arrives. Click to enlarge.</div></div><details style=\"margin:-10px 0 18px;font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.6;\"><summary style=\"cursor:pointer;color:var(--accent-cyan);\">Read what happens in this clip</summary><ol style=\"margin:0 0 12px 20px;font-size:14px;line-height:1.7;\"><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\">The collection folder starts empty. The helper program, <code>collect.js</code>, adds the guide pages to the list.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\">The harvester fetches each file in turn and pauses between requests, so it isn't hammering the site.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\">Each line reports the file, its size, its picture dimensions if it has any, and its fingerprint (<code>sha256</code>).</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\">The folder listing grows with every download.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\">It finishes with 10 files and writes a receipt.</li></ol></details><div class=\"callout\"><strong>What is that sha256 thing?</strong>It is a <strong>fingerprint</strong> of a file: a short code computed from its contents. Change a single letter and the code changes completely. The receipt records one for each file, so later on we can prove exactly which version of each page our facts came from.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">What we have at the end</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">A private folder on your computer, with no game in it yet. Everything here was put there by programs, and you didn't download anything by hand:</p><pre class=\"code-snippet\"><code>ember-quest/collection/\n  EmberQuest-AshfordTown.png      &lt;- town map picture\n  EmberQuest-EmberfallCave.png    &lt;- cave map picture\n  EmberQuest-World.png            &lt;- world map picture\n  guide/weapons.html              &lt;- the main price list\n  guide/shopkeeper-notes.html     &lt;- a player's notes (is it right?)\n  guide/anniversary-prices.html   &lt;- about a remake (a trap!)\n  guide/bestiary.html             &lt;- monster chart\n  guide/chests.html               &lt;- treasure list\n  guide/magic.html                &lt;- spell table\n  guide/walkthrough.html          &lt;- the story\n  sources-manifest.json           &lt;- the shopping list\n  download-receipt.json           &lt;- proof of what we got</code></pre><p class=\"lesson-text\">Notice that two of those pages are suspicious. One is a player's memory of the prices. One describes a different version of the game. A careless program would take them at face value. Module 4 shows how the parser catches both.</p></div>",
          "labSteps": [
            "Create the practice website",
            "Start the website, and look at it",
            "Have the harvester make the shopping list",
            "Add the guide pages and download everything"
          ],
          "labDetails": [
            {
              "title": "Create the practice website",
              "who": "Your AI agent runs a program",
              "description": "There is no website yet, so a program writes one. You won't need to go anywhere on the internet.",
              "agentPrompt": "Using packages/robos-jrpg-parser/demo/README.md, create the Ember Quest practice website in ~/ember-quest/site. Tell me what you ran and what it created.",
              "explain": "A JavaScript program, `practice-site.js`, writes the made-up fan site's pages and map pictures into a new folder called `ember-quest` in your home folder. Nothing is downloaded, and nothing goes online.",
              "code": "node packages/robos-jrpg-parser/demo/practice-site.js ~/ember-quest/site",
              "expected": "Your agent reports it wrote the practice site. The folder holds an `atlas` folder of maps and a `guide` folder of pages."
            },
            {
              "title": "Start the website, and look at it",
              "who": "Your AI agent starts it, then you look",
              "description": "A folder of pages isn't a website until something serves it. Once this is running, open http://127.0.0.1:8765/atlas/ in your normal browser. You're only looking: don't download anything.",
              "agentPrompt": "Following packages/robos-jrpg-parser/demo/README.md, start the practice website on port 8765 and leave it running in the background. Give me the address when it is ready.",
              "explain": "A tiny web server, `serve.js`, starts showing that folder as a website. Only your own computer can see it. It keeps running until it's stopped, which is why it runs in the background, and it notes every request it gets.",
              "code": "# This keeps running, so type later commands in a second terminal window\nnode packages/robos-jrpg-parser/demo/serve.js ~/ember-quest/site 8765",
              "expected": "Your browser shows the map atlas with five maps from two games.",
              "image": "/assets/elearning/jrpg-kgraph/demo/atlas-keep-and-ignore.png",
              "imageAlt": "The map atlas page with the three Ember Quest maps marked keep and two Star Knight maps marked ignore",
              "caption": "The site as seen in the clip. The green and red marks were added for the recording, so your copy won't have them."
            },
            {
              "title": "Have the harvester make the shopping list",
              "who": "Your AI agent runs a program",
              "description": "This is the first half of scraping: working out what to download. Nothing gets downloaded yet.",
              "agentPrompt": "Following packages/robos-jrpg-parser/demo/README.md, run the harvester against the practice site's atlas page in discover-only mode, keeping only files that start with EmberQuest-, and save into ~/ember-quest/collection. Then show me the list of files it found.",
              "explain": "The harvester, `harvest_jrpg_assets.py`, fetches that one page, reads its links, keeps the ones starting with `EmberQuest-`, and writes them to a file called `sources-manifest.json`.",
              "code": "python3 packages/robos-jrpg-parser/tools/harvest_jrpg_assets.py \\\n  --out-dir ~/ember-quest/collection \\\n  --vgmaps-index http://127.0.0.1:8765/atlas/ \\\n  --base-url http://127.0.0.1:8765/atlas/ \\\n  --map-prefix EmberQuest- \\\n  --discover-only",
              "expected": "Three maps on the list, out of the five on the page. The output says `(3 map PNGs, 0 wiki pages)`. The \"0 wiki pages\" is fine, because the next step adds the guide pages.",
              "image": "/assets/elearning/jrpg-kgraph/demo/harvester-shopping-list.png",
              "imageAlt": "The harvester reporting three maps found, followed by a list of the three Ember Quest files",
              "caption": "The same step in the recording."
            },
            {
              "title": "Add the guide pages and download everything",
              "who": "Your AI agent runs two programs",
              "description": "This is the second half of scraping: actually fetching the files.",
              "agentPrompt": "Following packages/robos-jrpg-parser/demo/README.md, add the guide pages to the manifest in ~/ember-quest/collection, then have the harvester download everything on it. Tell me how many files arrived, and show me what is in the folder.",
              "explain": "Two programs run. `collect.js` adds the seven guide pages to the list. Then the harvester fetches every file on the list from your practice website, waiting half a second between each, and writes a receipt with a fingerprint of each one.",
              "code": "node packages/robos-jrpg-parser/demo/collect.js ~/ember-quest/collection\n\npython3 packages/robos-jrpg-parser/tools/harvest_jrpg_assets.py \\\n  --out-dir ~/ember-quest/collection \\\n  --manifest ~/ember-quest/collection/sources-manifest.json",
              "expected": "`The manifest now lists 3 maps and 7 guide pages.`, then ten lines starting `[DOWNLOADED]`, then `[DONE] Verified 10 files`. The folder ends up with 12 files: those 10, plus the list and the receipt. Ask your agent to run it again: the lines say `[CACHED]`, because the harvester doesn't fetch what it already has.",
              "image": "/assets/elearning/jrpg-kgraph/demo/download-finished.png",
              "imageAlt": "The harvester having downloaded ten files, with the collection folder listing all of them",
              "caption": "Ten files, each with its fingerprint."
            }
          ],
          "quiz": [
            {
              "question": "In the hands-on steps of this module, how do the Ember Quest files get onto your computer?",
              "options": [
                "You ask your agent to run the harvester, which downloads them from a practice website on your own computer",
                "You open real fan websites in a browser, and you save each of the pages and map pictures yourself by hand",
                "You pay for a download from the game's publisher, and then you unpack the files into the collection folder",
                "Your agent searches the whole internet for fan sites and then copies everything that it manages to find"
              ],
              "answer": "You ask your agent to run the harvester, which downloads them from a practice website on your own computer",
              "explanation": "In this course a program does the downloading, from a site that only exists on your machine. On a real game it is the other way around: a person collects files by hand, and the harvester refuses to connect to real sites."
            },
            {
              "question": "What is the manifest that the harvester writes before downloading anything?",
              "options": [
                "It is a shopping list of the exact files to fetch, so that we can check the list before downloading",
                "It is a legal document that gives the harvester permission to make copies of files from a website",
                "It is a compressed archive that contains every map and every guide page that the site has to offer",
                "It is a report written by the website itself that lists which of its pages have been visited lately"
              ],
              "answer": "It is a shopping list of the exact files to fetch, so that we can check the list before downloading",
              "explanation": "Discovery and downloading are separate steps. The list lets you see exactly what would be fetched, and it can be reused or edited."
            },
            {
              "question": "The atlas page lists five maps, but the harvester only keeps three. How does it decide?",
              "options": [
                "It opens each map and compares the picture with other maps of the game that it has already seen",
                "It asks an AI model to look at the thumbnails and judge which ones appear to belong to Ember Quest",
                "It keeps the three newest files on the page, based on the date that each one was last uploaded",
                "It keeps only the links whose file names start with the prefix we gave it, which is EmberQuest-"
              ],
              "answer": "It keeps only the links whose file names start with the prefix we gave it, which is EmberQuest-",
              "explanation": "A plain name rule does the work. It is predictable, and anyone can read the list afterwards and see exactly why each file is on it."
            },
            {
              "question": "What is the purpose of the sha256 fingerprint recorded for each downloaded file?",
              "options": [
                "It encrypts each file so that nobody else is able to open it without first entering a password",
                "It lets us prove later exactly which version of a file our facts came from, as any change alters it",
                "It tells the website who downloaded the file, so that the owner of the site is able to contact us",
                "It shrinks each downloaded file so that the whole collection takes up less room on the hard drive"
              ],
              "answer": "It lets us prove later exactly which version of a file our facts came from, as any change alters it",
              "explanation": "A fingerprint is computed from a file's contents. If even one letter changes, so does the fingerprint, which makes it good evidence of what we actually collected."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "mod-04-reading",
          "title": "Module 4: Stages 2 and 3, Reading and Organizing. From a Pile of Files to Facts",
          "durationMinutes": 20,
          "overview": "Your agent runs the parser. Watch it sort the pile, set aside a page about the wrong game, catch two sources disagreeing about a price, and report what is still missing. Then see the facts become a connected knowledge graph.",
          "lessonHtml": "<div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">The problem with what people write</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">Our folder is full of pages written for people, and people are inconsistent. They disagree. They write about other versions of a game. They misremember. If a program copied everything without thinking, the result would be confidently wrong.</p><p class=\"lesson-text\">So the parser doesn't just read. It does four separate jobs:</p><div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;margin:14px 0;align-items:stretch;\"><div style=\"flex:1 1 150px;background:var(--bg-card);border:1px solid var(--border);border-top:3px solid var(--accent);border-radius:8px;padding:10px 12px;\"><div style=\"font-size:12px;color:var(--accent-cyan);font-weight:700;\">Job 1</div><div style=\"font-weight:700;color:var(--text-bright);margin:2px 0 4px;\">Sort</div><div style=\"font-size:12.5px;line-height:1.5;\">Work out what each file is, and set aside anything about the wrong game.</div></div><div style=\"flex:1 1 150px;background:var(--bg-card);border:1px solid var(--border);border-top:3px solid var(--accent);border-radius:8px;padding:10px 12px;\"><div style=\"font-size:12px;color:var(--accent-cyan);font-weight:700;\">Job 2</div><div style=\"font-weight:700;color:var(--text-bright);margin:2px 0 4px;\">Read tables</div><div style=\"font-size:12.5px;line-height:1.5;\">Copy numbers exactly, using ordinary code.</div></div><div style=\"flex:1 1 150px;background:var(--bg-card);border:1px solid var(--border);border-top:3px solid var(--accent);border-radius:8px;padding:10px 12px;\"><div style=\"font-size:12px;color:var(--accent-cyan);font-weight:700;\">Job 3</div><div style=\"font-weight:700;color:var(--text-bright);margin:2px 0 4px;\">Compare</div><div style=\"font-size:12.5px;line-height:1.5;\">Flag it when two sources disagree.</div></div><div style=\"flex:1 1 150px;background:var(--bg-card);border:1px solid var(--border);border-top:3px solid var(--accent);border-radius:8px;padding:10px 12px;\"><div style=\"font-size:12px;color:var(--accent-cyan);font-weight:700;\">Job 4</div><div style=\"font-weight:700;color:var(--text-bright);margin:2px 0 4px;\">Report gaps</div><div style=\"font-size:12.5px;line-height:1.5;\">List what's still missing, in plain English.</div></div></div></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Who does what in this module</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">Your agent runs one program, the <strong>parser</strong> (<code>jrpg-parser.js</code>), three times, each with a different command. You read the results.</p><div style=\"overflow-x:auto;margin:12px 0;\"><table style=\"border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.5;\"><thead><tr><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">Command</th><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">What it does</th><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">What it reads</th><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">What it creates</th><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">Goes online?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\"><code>scan</code></td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Sorts the files and says what each one is</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Your collection folder</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Nothing. It just prints a list</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">No</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\"><code>parse</code></td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Pulls out facts and builds the knowledge graph</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Your collection folder</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A <code>kgraph</code> folder, holding <code>parsed.json</code> and the graph</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">No</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\"><code>coverage</code></td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Reports what is covered and what is missing</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">The <code>kgraph</code> folder</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Nothing. It just prints a report</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">No</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Watch it: the whole process</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">Follow one single fact through this clip: <strong>the price of the Iron Sword</strong>.</p><div class=\"doc-figure\"><img src=\"/assets/elearning/jrpg-kgraph/demo/parse.gif\" alt=\"Screen recording: the weapons page shows the Iron Sword at 40 gold. The parser sorts the files and skips a remake page that says 99. A player notes page says 45, and the parser reports the disagreement. The Iron Sword then appears as a structured fact with a price of 40.\" class=\"doc-img\" loading=\"lazy\" onclick=\"openLightbox(this.src, this.alt)\"><div class=\"doc-caption\">One price, three pages, three different answers, and how the parser handles each. Click to enlarge.</div></div><details style=\"margin:-10px 0 18px;font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.6;\"><summary style=\"cursor:pointer;color:var(--accent-cyan);\">Read what happens in this clip</summary><ol style=\"margin:0 0 12px 20px;font-size:14px;line-height:1.7;\"><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\">The main guide says the Iron Sword costs 40 gold.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\">The <code>scan</code> command sorts the files. The page about the Anniversary Edition remake is marked <code>skip</code>, because its prices belong to a different game. It says 99.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\">The <code>parse</code> command reads every table it can.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\">A player's notes page says 45. The parser keeps 40 from the main guide, and writes down the disagreement for a human to look at.</li><li style=\"margin-bottom:6px;\">The Iron Sword is now a structured fact: price 40, with a note of the page it came from.</li></ol></details></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Job 1: Sorting the pile</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">The <code>scan</code> command looks at each file's type, picture size, name and page title, then gives its verdict. Here are a few of its lines:</p><pre class=\"code-snippet\"><code>use   area-map    256x192   EmberQuest-AshfordTown.png\nuse   world-map   256x192   EmberQuest-World.png\nskip  document              guide/anniversary-prices.html\n      (Title names another edition or a mod, so its stats and prices are not used.)\nuse   walkthrough           guide/walkthrough.html</code></pre><p class=\"lesson-text\">It spotted the remake because the page title mentions an \"Anniversary Edition\". Without this step, the shop in our rebuilt game would charge 99 gold for a sword that should cost 40.</p></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Job 2: Reading tables with code, not AI</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">It's tempting to hand everything to an AI and ask it to read. We don't, for tables. AI models can quietly change a number, and they may \"remember\" facts from other versions of a game rather than reading what's on the page. Tables go through ordinary code, which finds the column called \"Price\" and copies exactly what it says.</p><p class=\"lesson-text\">AI is useful for flowing paragraphs, like a walkthrough, where the facts aren't in neat rows. So the parser can call an AI model itself for that part, and it works with GitHub Copilot, Claude Code, Codex, or Gemini and Antigravity. That's where our safety check comes in.</p><div class=\"callout\"><strong>The quote check: no quote, no fact</strong>When the AI reads a paragraph, it must also hand back the <strong>exact sentence</strong> that supports each fact. Our code then searches the original file for that sentence, word for word. If it isn't there, the fact is thrown away. It's like asking a student to cite the page, then actually turning to that page to look. On the real Final Fantasy run, this check threw out 4 facts the AI could not back up.</div></div><div class=\"callout warn\"><strong>Two different AIs: don't mix them up</strong><strong>Your agent</strong> is the assistant you talk to. It runs the parser for you. <strong>The parser's AI step</strong> is a separate call the parser makes while reading paragraphs. The <code>--no-agent</code> option in the labs turns off only that second one. It means \"plain code only, no AI needed, no cost\", and has nothing to do with the agent you're chatting with.</div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Job 3: Catching disagreements</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">This line really did come out of the parser in the clip:</p><pre class=\"code-snippet\"><code>Iron Sword: price is 40 in guide/weapons.html but 45 in guide/shopkeeper-notes.html.</code></pre><p class=\"lesson-text\">Notice what it <em>doesn't</em> do. It doesn't quietly pick one, or average them to 42. It uses the page written for this version of the game, and leaves a clear note saying who disagreed. A person can then settle it. Real guides do this constantly: Final Fantasy's sources disagreed on 5 equipment numbers, such as whether one sword costs 5,000 or 6,000 gold.</p></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Job 4: Knowing what you don't know</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">The most valuable output might be the list of what's missing. The <code>coverage</code> report compares what we have to what a game needs. Here is a shortened version:</p><pre class=\"code-snippet\"><code>Ember Quest (Retro (1989)): 25 nodes\n  ok         1  World map reference\n  ok         2  Town, castle &amp; dungeon map references\n  MISSING    0  Places\n  MISSING    3  Weapons\n  ok         2  Spells\n  MISSING    0  Shops &amp; inns\n  MISSING    0  Story milestones\n\nStill to collect by hand:\n  - a walkthrough that names each place\n  - a weapon table with stats and prices\n  - a track list saying where each track plays</code></pre><div class=\"callout warn\"><strong>Why does it say MISSING with 3 weapons?</strong>The bar is set for a full-size game, and Ember Quest is deliberately tiny. Several others are empty because the labs skip the AI step (<code>--no-agent</code>), so nothing reads the walkthrough's paragraphs. For a real game, this list becomes your to-do list. It's far better for a tool to say \"I don't have this\" than to guess.</div></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Stage 3: Organizing into a knowledge graph</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">Finally, the facts are saved as connected nodes. Here is the real node for the Iron Sword, with a few bookkeeping lines left out:</p><pre class=\"code-snippet\"><code>{\n  \"@id\": \"urn:robos:jrpg:ember-quest:item:iron-sword\",\n  \"dcterms:title\": \"Iron Sword\",\n  \"robos:itemCategory\": \"weapon\",\n  \"jrpg:usableBy\": [\"Warden\"],\n  \"robos:attackBonus\": 8,\n  \"robos:cost\": 40,\n  \"jrpg:source\": { \"@id\": \"urn:robos:jrpg:ember-quest:source:guide-weapons-html\" }\n}</code></pre><div style=\"overflow-x:auto;margin:12px 0;\"><table style=\"border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.5;\"><thead><tr><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">This line</th><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">Means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\"><code>@id</code></td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A unique name for this item, so nothing can be confused with it. Like a passport number.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\"><code>dcterms:title</code></td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Its human-readable name.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\"><code>jrpg:usableBy</code></td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A link to the class that can use it.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\"><code>robos:attackBonus</code>, <code>robos:cost</code></td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Its damage and its price.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\"><code>jrpg:source</code></td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A link to the source file it came from.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"lesson-text\">Ember Quest's graph has just 25 of these nodes. Final Fantasy's has 1,206. They work in exactly the same way.</p></div>",
          "labSteps": [
            "Sort the pile with scan",
            "Parse it into facts",
            "Find the disagreement",
            "Read the report of what is missing"
          ],
          "labDetails": [
            {
              "title": "Sort the pile with scan",
              "who": "Your AI agent runs a program, then you read the result",
              "description": "See what the parser makes of each file you collected.",
              "agentPrompt": "Following packages/robos-jrpg-parser/demo/README.md, run the parser's scan on ~/ember-quest/collection. Tell me in plain English which files it will use, which it skips, and why.",
              "explain": "The parser looks at each file's type, picture size, name and page title, then prints a verdict for each. It changes nothing.",
              "code": "node packages/robos-jrpg-parser/bin/jrpg-parser.js scan ~/ember-quest/collection",
              "expected": "`guide/anniversary-prices.html` is skipped, because its title names another edition of the game. The two `.json` files are also skipped, because they are the harvester's own paperwork.",
              "image": "/assets/elearning/jrpg-kgraph/demo/scan-sets-remake-aside.png",
              "imageAlt": "The scan output with the anniversary prices page marked skip, beside that page showing a price of 99",
              "caption": "The remake says 99 gold, and the parser won't use it."
            },
            {
              "title": "Parse it into facts",
              "who": "Your AI agent runs a program",
              "description": "This does the actual reading and builds the knowledge graph.",
              "agentPrompt": "Following packages/robos-jrpg-parser/demo/README.md, parse ~/ember-quest/collection into ~/ember-quest/kgraph with the title Ember Quest and the edition Retro (1989), using the --no-agent option. Tell me what it found.",
              "explain": "The parser reads every table with ordinary code, compares sources, and writes the facts to `parsed.json` and the knowledge graph in a new `kgraph` folder. `--no-agent` turns off its own AI step, so it costs nothing and needs no account.",
              "code": "node packages/robos-jrpg-parser/bin/jrpg-parser.js parse ~/ember-quest/collection \\\n  --out ~/ember-quest/kgraph \\\n  --title \"Ember Quest\" --edition \"Retro (1989)\" --no-agent",
              "expected": "A line per page, such as `guide/bestiary.html: charts have 3 enemies`, and a report starting `Ember Quest (Retro (1989)): 25 nodes`."
            },
            {
              "title": "Find the disagreement",
              "who": "Your AI agent reads a file for you",
              "description": "The parser wrote down where sources disagreed. Have your agent find it.",
              "agentPrompt": "Did any of my sources disagree? Show me the conflicts section of ~/ember-quest/kgraph/parsed.json and explain it in plain English.",
              "explain": "No program runs here apart from a search of the file. Your agent is just reading the parser's output for you.",
              "code": "grep -A2 '\"conflicts\"' ~/ember-quest/kgraph/parsed.json",
              "expected": "`Iron Sword: price is 40 in guide/weapons.html but 45 in guide/shopkeeper-notes.html.` Ask your agent which one the game will use, and why. It should say 40, from the main guide.",
              "image": "/assets/elearning/jrpg-kgraph/demo/parser-flags-conflict.png",
              "imageAlt": "A player notes page showing 45 gold, with the parser reporting that the price is 40 in one file and 45 in another",
              "caption": "The disagreement as seen in the recording."
            },
            {
              "title": "Read the report of what is missing",
              "who": "Your AI agent runs a program, then you read the result",
              "description": "If you were doing this for real, this report would be your to-do list.",
              "agentPrompt": "Following packages/robos-jrpg-parser/demo/README.md, show me the parser's coverage report for ~/ember-quest/kgraph, and explain what is missing and why.",
              "explain": "The parser compares what it has with what a full game needs, and prints the gaps. It changes nothing.",
              "code": "node packages/robos-jrpg-parser/bin/jrpg-parser.js coverage --out ~/ember-quest/kgraph",
              "expected": "A list of `ok` and `MISSING` lines, then \"Still to collect by hand\" and \"Notes\". **This does not mean your download failed.** You will see lines like `MISSING 3 Weapons` and be told to collect a weapon table, even though you just did. The bar is set for a full-size game, at least 10 weapons, and Ember Quest only has 3. Other sections are empty because the parser's AI step was off.",
              "image": "/assets/elearning/jrpg-kgraph/demo/iron-sword-as-data.png",
              "imageAlt": "The Iron Sword shown as a structured fact with its damage, price and source page",
              "caption": "The Iron Sword, now a fact that programs can use."
            }
          ],
          "quiz": [
            {
              "question": "The Iron Sword is 40 gold on the guide, 45 in a player's notes and 99 on a remake page. What does the parser do?",
              "options": [
                "It takes an average of all three numbers, so that the final price is fair to all of the sources",
                "It uses 99 because that was the most recently written page and so is probably the most accurate",
                "It skips the remake, keeps 40 from the main guide, and writes down the 45 as a flagged disagreement",
                "It stops the entire process with an error, and refuses to continue until a person fixes the pages"
              ],
              "answer": "It skips the remake, keeps 40 from the main guide, and writes down the 45 as a flagged disagreement",
              "explanation": "Each kind of problem gets its own treatment. A different game is set aside, and a real disagreement is recorded for a person while the most trustworthy source wins."
            },
            {
              "question": "Why are number tables read with ordinary code rather than by an AI model?",
              "options": [
                "Ordinary code copies exactly what the column says, whereas an AI may change or \"remember\" a number",
                "AI models are not able to read any kind of table at all, because tables are stored in special files",
                "Ordinary code is much more creative, so it fills in any numbers that happen to be missing from a table",
                "AI models are only available to very large companies and cannot be used on a home computer at all"
              ],
              "answer": "Ordinary code copies exactly what the column says, whereas an AI may change or \"remember\" a number",
              "explanation": "For exact numbers, you want a tool that can only copy. An AI that has read about many versions of a game might give a plausible number from the wrong one."
            },
            {
              "question": "How does the quote check stop an AI model from inventing facts?",
              "options": [
                "It asks the AI model the same question three times, and only keeps those answers that all match",
                "It sends each of the facts to a person, who must read them all and approve them one after another",
                "It compares the facts with what other fans have said about the game on the wider public internet",
                "The AI must return the exact supporting sentence, and code checks that it is really in the source file"
              ],
              "answer": "The AI must return the exact supporting sentence, and code checks that it is really in the source file",
              "explanation": "Like checking a citation by turning to the page. If the sentence is not literally in the file, the fact is dropped."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "mod-05-rebuilding",
          "title": "Module 5: Stage 4, Rebuilding. Turning Facts into a Playable Game",
          "durationMinutes": 20,
          "overview": "How a picture of a map becomes a grid of numbers a game can walk on, how a price in a table becomes a real shop, and where new artwork comes in. Then play the result yourself.",
          "lessonHtml": "<div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">Who does what in this module</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">Your agent runs one new program, then you play the result in your own browser.</p><div style=\"overflow-x:auto;margin:12px 0;\"><table style=\"border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.5;\"><thead><tr><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">The program</th><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">What it does</th><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">What it reads</th><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">What it creates</th><th style=\"text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;color:var(--text-bright);\">Goes online?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\"><strong>build-game.js</strong></td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Cuts the collected maps into numbered tiles, and combines them with the parsed facts into a playable game</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">Your collection folder and <code>parsed.json</code></td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">A <code>game</code> folder you can play in a browser</td><td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:7px 10px;vertical-align:top;\">No</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div><div class=\"lesson-section\"><h4 class=\"lesson-subtitle\">The big idea: a map is a grid of numbers</h4><p class=\"lesson-text\">Look at this picture of Ashford Town. To you, it's a village. To a game, a picture is useless: it can't tell a path from a pond. So we cut the picture into its 16-by-16-pixel tiles, give each different tile a number, and write out the whole town as a grid.</p><div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:18px;align-items:center;justify-content:center;margin:14px 0;\"><div style=\"text-align:center;\"><img src=\"/assets/elearning/jrpg-kgraph/demo/EmberQuest-AshfordTown.png\" alt=\"The map of Ashford Town: a town with a shop, a pond, a chest, a road and a cave, surrounded by trees\" style=\"width:384px;max-width:100%;image-rendering:pixelated;border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:6px;\"><div class=\"doc-caption\">The picture we collected</div></div><div style=\"text-align:center;\"><div style=\"overflow-x:auto;\"><table style=\"border-collapse:collapse;margin:0 auto;font:12px monospace;\" aria-label=\"Ashford Town as a grid of tile numbers\"><tr><td style=\"width:24px;height:24px;text-align:center;padding:0;border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.35);background:#1f5e34;color:#fff;font-weight:700;\">0</td><td style=\"width:24px;height:24px;text-align:center;padding:0;border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.35);background:#1f5e34;color:#fff;font-weight:700;\">0</td><td style=\"width:24px;height:24px;text-align:center;padding:0;border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.35);background:#1f5e34;color:#fff;font-weight:700;\">0</td><td style=\"width:24px;height:24px;text-align:center;padding:0;border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.35);background:#1f5e34;color:#fff;font-weight:700;\">0</td><td style=\"width:24px;height:24px;text-align:center;padding:0;border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.35);background:#1f5e34;color:#fff;font-weight:700;\">0</td><td style=\"width:24px;height:24px;text-align:center;padding:0;border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.35);background:#1f5e34;color:#fff;font-weight:700;\">0</td><td style=\"width:24px;height:24px;text-align:center;padding:0;border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.35);background:#1f5e34;color:#fff;font-weight:700;\">0</td><td style=\"width:24px;height:24px;text-align:center;padding:0;border: